 reconcile-before-dispatch |\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-34-| dagger | Content-addressed execution DAG | MEDIUM | ADAPT_IDEA | Recursive call-digest identity → deterministic memoized execution |\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-35-| opentelemetry-collector | Telemetry pipeline semantics | LOW | ADAPT_IDEA | Per-consumer MutatesData declarations + reverse-topological startup |\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-36-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-37----\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-38-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-39-## Architecture directives for the GNOSIS kernel\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-40-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-41-The strongest cross-repo convergences, stated as implementable kernel rules. Each directive is evidenced by multiple independent codebases that arrived at the same mechanism.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-42-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-43-### 1. One canonical-bytes hash primitive under every chain\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-44-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-45-**Evidence:** bernstein (journal/WAL/work ledger/lineage share one `canonical_json` contract), opentraces (event_id = sha256 of canonical JSON including sequence + previous pointer), agent-capsule (canonical JSON → SHA3-256 → signed), reprise (`canonical_bytes`/`blob_hash`), agent-governance-toolkit (action identity = sha256 over key-sorted canonical JSON), dagger (recursive call digests).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-46-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:47:**Rule:** The kernel defines exactly one primitive — `canonical_json = dumps(sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\",\":\")).encode(\"utf-8\")` — and every hash in the system (journal entries, event ids, replay keys, action identities, approval bindings) derives from it. Document the contract so a third party can re-derive any hash by hand. Bernstein's warning applies: chain proliferation without a shared contract (five parallel event systems) is the failure mode to design out from day one.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-48-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-49-### 2. Append-only, hash-chained journal with fail-closed recovery\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-50-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:51:**Evidence:** bernstein (`Journal._validate_chain_for_recovery` recomputes every hash from genesis, never trusts the tail), opentraces (CAS append to a git ref with bounded retry; content-addressed ids make replay order tamper-evident), agent-workspace-fabric (`WorkspaceEvent` append-only, per-workspace sequence counter, \"no UPDATE/DELETE ever\"), cezar (per-run NDJSON append + atomic tmp+rename index).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-52-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-53-**Rule:** Kernel events are append-only rows with a per-scope monotonic sequence and a previous-hash pointer. On open, recompute the whole chain: a torn final line is a legitimate crash (keep the prefix); any interior corruption, sequence gap, or prev-hash break refuses to open and names the offending line. Never extend a chain from an unverified anchor.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-54-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-55-### 3. State machine as a single frozen allow-list, with gates projected from it\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-56-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-57-**Evidence:** agent-workspace-fabric (`MappingProxyType` of `frozenset` transitions + `assert_transition` typed errors + documented salvage edges), symphony (orchestrator is the *only* component that mutates scheduling state; SPEC §7 vocabulary), bernstein (`approval_gate.py` derives approvable/completable sets from `tasks/lifecycle.py` constants so no transport gets weaker rules), spec-kit (phase-gated pipeline with typed handoffs), cezar (review park is a store-owned state, not a prompt convention).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-58-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-59-**Rule:** Allowed Task transitions live once, in one importable kernel module, as immutable data. Every surface (CLI, MCP, HTTP, adapter) imports them; illegal transitions raise a typed error carrying `(from, to, allowed_next)`. The kernel is the *sole writer* of status — structurally, not by convention (AWF's guard-by-convention gap is the anti-pattern). Include explicit salvage edges for restart recovery, and gate spec→plan→tasks→implement phases as first-class states so no Task reaches implementation without a versioned spec artifact upstream.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-60-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-61-### 4. Two-plane ownership: durable claims + ephemeral fenced leases\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-62-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-63-**Evidence:** beads (claim = CAS on a `row_lock` version token, in the same transaction that computed readiness; leases/heartbeats in a non-replicated ephemeral table; TTL reclaim with recovery events), agent-workspace-fabric (fencing epochs bumped as a SQL expression so stale workers are fenced on their next CAS), smithers (durable rewind lease with `checkStillHeld` re-checked *inside* the transaction), grit (`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` atomic check-then-set symbol leases — plus the fencing hole GNOSIS must close), cezar (repo-root promise-chain lease; account-scoped holds).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-64-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-65-**Rule:** Separate history-worthy ownership transitions (durable, versioned) from liveness chatter (ephemeral leases + heartbeats that never enter history). Claims are conditional CAS on a version token rewritten by every ownership mutation, idempotent for same-actor retries, returning typed conflict data (holder, status) — never prose. Fencing epochs increment monotonically inside the claim transaction; expired leases are reclaimed by TTL sweep that repeats the expiry predicate at delete time. A lease stays bound to unresolved integration state — never release it just because a merge failed (grit's mistake).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-66-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-67-### 5. Worktree lifecycle with provenance-gated destruction\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-68-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-69-**Evidence:** agent-workspace-fabric (per-repo bare mirror + per-workspace worktree, idempotent removal, cleanup that deliberately avoids blanket `reset --hard`), cezar (idempotent worktree creation, never deletes non-empty unregistered dirs, autosave refuses mid-merge/conflict-marker states and tags its reason), orca (`BranchMode.Created` gate — only branches orca minted may be auto-deleted; resume header validated as hostile input before any destructive git action), grit (merge under PID-liveness-checked lock, preserves branch+worktree on failure), bernstein (content-addressed snapshots under refs, fork refuses on sha mismatch).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-70-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-71-**Rule:** The kernel mints per-task worktrees/branches and records that provenance at creation time; destruction is gated on \"did the kernel mint this\". Creation is idempotent with reattach paths; non-empty unregistered directories are recoverable work, never deleted. Autosave commits carry a reason tag and refuse conflict-marker states — losing a recovery point beats poisoning the branch. Any resume/recovery header is validated as hostile input (ref-shape checks, protected-branch floor, cross-checks) before any destructive operation; invalid state is a hard abort, and corrupt-state is distinguished from absent-state.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-72-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-73-### 6. Review convergence: signal ∧ evidence, tri-state rounds, stalemate fingerprints, nothing-lost ledger\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-74-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-75-**Evidence:** ralphex (ALL_TASKS_DONE discarded if unchecked boxes remain; DONE means \"zero issues this round\", not \"I finished fixing\"; git HEAD+diff fingerprint stalemate counter), orca (bounded review loop with hard caps, per-severity confidence gates, and a `GateLedger` where every gated-out or declined finding is carried and printed at exit), cezar (review park state the agent cannot bypass; done-signals parsed only from the agent's own turn text), agent-arena (independent generation → evidence checking → blind judging, dissent preserved in the synthesis artifact).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-76-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-77-**Rule:** An agent's DONE claim never closes a Task. The kernel re-checks a durable artifact (plan checkboxes, git HEAD/diff fingerprint) and downgrades signal-without-evidence to continue-with-warning. Rounds are tri-state: clean round → converged; fixed-something → schedule another independent verification round; can't-fix → typed failure. A stalemate counter over repo-state fingerprints (N consecutive unchanged rounds → `STALEMATE`) is a kernel circuit breaker, resets on change, and never counts when evidence collection itself failed. Every finding held back by a gate is carried in a ledger and surfaced at every loop exit — the loop cannot silently drop evidence. Dissent from independent reviewers is preserved in the evidence artifact.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-78-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:79:### 7. Strict replay as default; write-ahead intent before side effects\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-80-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:81:**Evidence:** bernstein (`DeterministicStore` — replay miss aborts, never silently falls through to a live model; per-key FIFO preserves call order and multiplicity; response-determining params folded into the key), reprise (occurrence-aware Nth-identical-request matching from a content-addressed SQLite blob store; strict-mode `ReplayMiss`), smithers (journal row with status `intended` + {sideEffect, idempotent, hasRevert, idempotencyKey} persisted *before* any tool executes; rewind safety becomes a decidable query), dagger (same recipe → same digest → memoized result), catacomb (recorded evidence as regression fixtures).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-82-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-83-**Rule:** Record every LLM/tool call keyed by a content hash of its response-determining parameters (model, provider, temperature, max_tokens, effort). Replay is occurrence-aware and strict: a miss is an abort; live fall-through is an explicit opt-in. Before any side-effecting tool executes, persist an `intended` journal row with side-effect provenance flags, so rewind/fork/compensation safety is answered by querying the journal, never by guessing. Golden runs become regression fixtures.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-84-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-85-### 8. Fail-closed, stateless policy verdicts; honesty about enforcement\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-86-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-87-**Evidence:** agent-governance-toolkit (ACS: complete JSON snapshot in → exactly one of allow/deny/warn/escalate/transform out; *every* runtime failure collapses to deny with a reserved `runtime_error:*` reason policies may not emit; unconfigured intervention point denies; undeclared tool denies; approvals bound to the exact action hash), agentjail (verdicts over parsed command intent — program/flags/resolved paths — with rule_id provenance), nono (kernel-enforced deny-by-default as the floor beneath advisory hooks), orca (enforcement honesty matrix: every backend restriction classified Hard/SandboxApprox/PromptOnly/Ignored, machine-checked in tests), opentraces (single three-way egress predicate; `unknown` is never coerced to safe).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-88-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-89-**Rule:** The kernel's Policy Engine is a pure, stateless function: adapters build a complete snapshot per intervention point; the engine returns exactly one verdict; every internal failure returns deny with a reserved reason namespace, so a deny-by-error is always distinguishable from a policy deny. Missing bindings and undeclared tools fail closed — configuration gaps are never consent. NEEDS_HUMAN escalations bind approval to the sha256 action identity of exactly what was evaluated (anti-TOCTOU). Decisions key on structured, parsed intent — never string pattern-matching with hardcoded bypass lists. And per adapter, maintain a machine-checked enforcement matrix recording whether each restriction is mechanically enforced, sandbox-approximated, prompt-only, or ignored — the kernel's least-privilege decisions consume honest data.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-90-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-91-### 9. Typed failure taxonomy end-to-end; rate limits as park states; recovery as reconcile\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-92-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-93-**Evidence:** cezar (evidence-ordered usage-limit parsing that records *which* evidence shape carried each classification and refuses to guess; durable `autoResumeAt`; deadline holds vs in-flight holds keyed by runner:credential; boot re-adoption that revives through the normal queue path or fails loudly), smithers (`waiting-quota` durable park state with `resetAtMs`; startup crash recovery from pre-mutation audit rows), ralphex (three-tier retry/limit/error taxonomy with signal-suppressed retries), agent-workspace-fabric (reason codes flowing exception → log → append-only event → failure taxonomy → scheduler policy, with a generated catalog as the drift gate), symphony (distinct terminal reasons Failed/TimedOut/Stalled/Canceled because \"retry logic and logs differ\"; reconcile-before-dispatch on every poll tick), beads (typed `ClaimConflictError` carrying holder+status).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-94-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-95-**Rule:** Failures are typed at the adapter boundary (structured exit/frame codes first; prose parsing only as a last-resort enricher, with the evidence grade recorded on every classification). Reason codes flow unchanged from exception to append-only event to scheduler policy — implementing GNOSIS rules 5–7 (`RATE_LIMITED != FAIL_CODE`; `FAIL_INFRA` doesn't penalize the agent). `RATE_LIMITED` is a durable park state carrying a reset timestamp, with account-scoped holds distinguishing \"window known shut — block everything on this credential\" from \"one resume is probing — block only new work\". Recovery is reconcile, not restore: durable state on the record, volatile timers rebuilt idempotently on every scheduler pump, plus a boot sweep that re-adopts every stranded run through normal paths or fails it loudly — never leaving a ghost.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-96-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-97----\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-98-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-99-## Contradictions and open questions\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-100-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-101-1. **Fail-open vs fail-closed evidence sealing.** Bernstein's completion evidence gate is deliberately fail-open (\"Evidence sealing must NEVER block… any error is caught and swallowed\") and agent-capsule's adapters are fail-open by design; ACS, opentraces' egress predicate, and GNOSIS Rule 2 demand fail-closed. The tension is availability (don't lose finished work over a sealing bug) vs integrity (no DONE without evidence). GNOSIS's constitution settles the default (fail closed), but the open question is the recovery path: what evidence would settle the cost is a chaos test measuring, under injected sealing failures, the rate of lost-but-recoverable work in a fail-closed design vs the rate of falsely-closed Tasks in a fail-open one.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-102-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:103:2. **Loop exhaustion semantics.** Ralphex soft-continues on max-iterations (\"continuing…\") and reads unchanged HEAD as convergence even when the reviewer may simply have failed to act; orca hard-caps with a nothing-lost ledger; AWF's PR monitor has *no* iteration/wall-clock terminal gate at all, delegating livelock to human-notification gates (in tension with GNOSIS Rule 8). Evidence to settle: measure, on a replayed corpus of review loops, how often exhaustion-with-flow-through ships unconverged defects vs how often hard-stop + NEEDS_HUMAN merely delays a run that would have converged.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-104-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-105-3. **Advisory vs enforced ownership.** Grit's symbol locks are pure convention (`cmd_done` commits everything touched and never diffs changes against held locks); orca enforces \"fan-out can read, never commit\" at compile time via capability tokens; the deep analyses converge on a kernel merge gate (diff hunks mapped to AST symbols, `FAIL_POLICY` if changed-symbols ⊄ claimed-symbols) that no surveyed repo actually implements. Evidence to settle: a multi-agent conflict-injection benchmark counting how many unclaimed-symbol mutations reach merge under advisory-only vs gate-enforced ownership, and the gate's false-positive rate on legitimate refactors.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-106-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-107-4. **Where failure classification lives.** Cezar and ralphex classify failures by regex over provider prose at the terminal error; AWF, ACS, and symphony type failures structurally at the boundary. Cezar's evidence-graded prose parsing is the best of its kind, but it is inherently provider-version-fragile. Evidence to settle: a per-backend audit of what fraction of real failure events carry structured codes (exit codes, JSON frames) vs prose-only — if structured coverage is high, prose parsing is demoted to enricher; where it is low, cezar's evidence-ordered recognizers are the fallback.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-108-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-109-5. **In-band signals vs kernel-owned state.** Ralphex matches `<<<RALPHEX:...>>>` anywhere in output (a model *quoting* the marker triggers it, defended only by prompt exhortation); cezar narrows to line-anchored markers parsed only from the agent's own turn text, never tool output; cezar's review gate goes further — the state is store-owned and no marker can bypass it. Evidence to settle: marker-quoting injection tests against each scheme; the survey already strongly suggests kernel-owned out-of-band state wins, with in-band markers acceptable only as hints that never directly transition state.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-110-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-111-6. **Destructive teardown discipline.** Orca performs `git reset --hard` + gated `clean` in failure teardown (safe only because its recovery header is validated as hostile first); cezar and AWF deliberately refuse blanket resets, preferring autosave commits and idempotent reattach. GNOSIS Rule 27 forbids reset-as-shortcut, but orca shows a disciplined, validated reset can make resume-in-place trivial. Evidence to settle: crash-injection runs comparing recovery-loss incidents (work destroyed by teardown) vs poisoned-workspace incidents (stale state corrupting the resumed run) under each discipline.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-112-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-113-7. **Storage substrate weight vs scale.** Beads (Dolt, 60+ migrations, proxy daemon) and AWF (Postgres + Compose stacks) carry heavy substrates for semantics that cezar, reprise, grit, and smithers deliver on SQLite/plain files; beads' claim scan is also deliberately unbounded over the ready set (acknowledged in claim.go). SQLite-V1 is the GNOSIS ADR baseline. Evidence to settle: a scheduler benchmark at GNOSIS's actual scale envelope (hundreds to low-thousands of Tasks) measuring whether the materialized-`is_blocked` + CAS-claim pattern on SQLite holds up before any heavier substrate is even discussed.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-114-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-115-8. **Rate-limit hold granularity.** Cezar's two-hold account model (deadline holds block everything on a credential; in-flight holds block only new work — each kind fixed a real bug in opposite directions) vs smithers' simpler single `waiting-quota` park. Evidence to settle: reproduce resume-stampede and resume-deadlock scenarios with multiple concurrent runs sharing one credential; if both reproduce, the two-hold model is required, not optional.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-116-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-117----\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-118-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-119-## Per-repository findings\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-120-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-121-*Deep analyses (10 repositories), followed by group sweeps (3 blocks). Sections are reproduced verbatim from the archaeology agents, evidence labels preserved.*\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-122-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:123:### bernstein — deterministic multi-CLI-agent orchestrator with hash-chained journals, signed lineage, and hermetic replay; the strongest kernel-authority reference found for GNOSIS\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-124-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-125-- **Identity:** https://github.com/sipyourdrink-ltd/bernstein @ 7dce402 (2026-08-18) | Apache-2.0, standard text, no riders (verbatim tail: \"Copyright 2026 Alex Chernysh\"; separate NOTICE + TRADEMARKS.md) | Python >=3.12 (dev-pinned 3.14), ~1,879 .py files / ~721k LOC in src, repo 114 MB | hatchling + uv (uv.lock; no custom hatch build hooks — VERIFIED via pyproject grep) | runtime: Click CLI, FastAPI task server, Textual TUI, MCP server, spawns 40+ external coding-agent CLIs\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-126-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — code is defensively written and local-first (servers default-bind 127.0.0.1 and refuse non-loopback without a token, `mcp/remote_transport.py:309` VERIFIED; telemetry default-off with DO_NOT_TRACK precedence, SELF-REPORTED docstring invariants; docker.sock mount commented out VERIFIED), but it is a ~720k-LOC system that spawns agent subprocesses by design, has 3 `pickle.loads` sites on local files (`cli/commands/cache_cmd.py:331`, `persistence/fingerprint.py:286`, `planning/duration_predictor.py:285` VERIFIED), and `benchmark/programbench.py:453-463` `exec()`s candidate code in-process (VERIFIED). Fine as READ-ONLY reference; unacceptable as a runtime dependency surface.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:127:- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — Bernstein independently converged on GNOSIS's exact constitution (kernel owns state/gates, agents propose, evidence before consensus) and implements the deepest journal/gate/replay machinery I have seen, with genuine Windows lanes (msvcrt lock paths, `newline=\"\"` byte-exact appends, PowerShell rmtree fallback, windows CI + `.github/windows-lane-baseline.json`). But adopting it as a dependency would import a monolith that violates GNOSIS Rule 30 (simplicity is a security property); the load-bearing mechanisms are each a few hundred lines of stdlib-only Python and their contracts are documented well enough to re-derive by hand. Reimplement the chain/replay/gate contracts natively in the GNOSIS kernel; keep bernstein as the audit oracle for design decisions.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-128-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-129-  - Hash-chained step journal with fail-closed recovery (`src/bernstein/core/persistence/journal.py:Journal`, `_validate_chain_for_recovery`): each step hashed as SHA256(canonical compact sorted-keys JSON of {prev_hash, input_hash, model, prompt, tool_call, tool_result, effort?}); on open the tip is recovered by **recomputing every hash from genesis, never trusting the tail**; a torn final line degrades gracefully but any interior corruption / seq gap / prev_hash break raises `JournalError` so a tampered chain cannot be extended from a poisoned anchor. Schema evolution by omission: `effort=None` keys byte-identically to legacy rows, so old chains re-verify unchanged. [VERIFIED — read full file]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:130:  - Hermetic deterministic replay (`src/bernstein/core/orchestration/deterministic.py:DeterministicStore`, `ReplayMissError`, `ReplayRecordingMissingError`; plus `replay/journal.py:EventJournal`): every LLM call recorded keyed by SHA256(model\\0prompt\\0provider\\0temperature\\0max_tokens); replay keeps a **per-key FIFO preserving call order and multiplicity**; a miss (including over-consumption) **aborts by default** instead of silently falling through to a live model, and whole-run replay refuses up front when no recording exists; live fall-through is an explicit env opt-in. `EventJournal` Merkle-chains all run events with **wall-clock excluded from the payload hash**, so byte-identical executions yield identical chains and divergence surfaces at a precise step index. [VERIFIED — read both files]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-131-  - Portable work ledger anchored in git refs (`src/bernstein/core/persistence/work_ledger.py`, `ledger_git.py`): the task graph and every transition form a hash chain (same canonical-JSON contract as the journal); **redaction runs before hashing** so the portable chain verifies anywhere without carrying cleartext; anchored under `refs/bernstein/work-ledger/<run-id>` as a **timestamp-free deterministic tree** (two operators anchoring the same chain get byte-identical trees); resume appends a nonce-bearing `run.resumed` entry so double-resume becomes structurally divergent and is caught at the next anchor, never silently merged. [VERIFIED — read module docstrings + hashing contract; internals NOT TESTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-132-  - Signed lineage transparency log + read-only CI gate (`src/bernstein/core/lineage/gate.py:check`): per-entry Ed25519 detached JWS verified against agent cards keyed by **(agent_id, kid)** (kid-substitution and key-rotation safe), optional operator HMAC, steward allowlist for merge entries, dangling-parent and unresolved-fork detection; verification binds to **exact on-disk bytes** (`canonicalise(entry) == raw_line`, strict `b\"\\n\"` splitting) to defeat re-canonicalisation and newline-reframing attacks. The gate is a pure function over a frozen log + cards dir — the reviewer never mutates what it judges. [VERIFIED — read full file]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-133-  - Decision WAL + gates projected from one state machine (`src/bernstein/core/persistence/wal.py:WALEntry` fsync-per-entry hash-chained decisions with `committed` flag and a rebuildable uncommitted-index cache; `src/bernstein/core/orchestration/phase_gates.py` deterministic stdlib rule runner R001–R005 with exactly-one bounded auto-repair re-fire; `src/bernstein/mcp/approval_gate.py` derives approvable/completable status sets **from `tasks/lifecycle.py` constants so no transport can obtain a weaker rule**). [VERIFIED — read wal.py head, phase_gates.py head, approval_gate.py head]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:134:  - Fork-from-step on git snapshots (`src/bernstein/core/replay/fork.py`): each step's snapshot is a content-addressed git commit under `refs/bernstein/snapshots/<run_id>/<N>`; forking cross-verifies the journal-recorded sha against the ref and **refuses on mismatch**, and the child run's first journal event parent-links the fork point, turning the run chain into a verifiable tree. [VERIFIED — read module docstring/flow; execution NOT TESTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:135:- **What GNOSIS should steal:** (1) The single canonical-bytes contract — one `canonical_json = dumps(sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\",\":\")).encode(\"utf-8\")` primitive shared by journal, WAL, work ledger and lineage, documented so a third party can re-derive any hash by hand; make it the GNOSIS Kernel's one chain primitive. (2) Fail-closed tail recovery semantics: recompute the whole chain on open, distinguish \"torn final line = legitimate crash, keep prefix\" from \"interior corruption = refuse and name the line\". (3) Strict-replay-as-default: a replay miss is an abort (`ReplayMissError`), never a silent live call, with response-determining params (model/provider/temperature/max_tokens/effort) folded into the replay key so parameter drift can't masquerade as a hit. (4) Gate authority projected from the state machine: allowed transitions live once in the task-lifecycle module and every surface (CLI/MCP/HTTP) imports them, so no entry point gets weaker rules; plus git-ref anchoring of the ledger with timestamp-free trees for portable, deterministic resume.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-136-- **What to avoid:** (1) The completion evidence gate is deliberately **fail-open** (`evidence/completion_gate.py`: \"Evidence sealing must NEVER block… any error is caught and swallowed\") — that directly contradicts GNOSIS Rule 2 (no DONE without evidence); GNOSIS's completion gate must fail closed. (2) Chain proliferation and monolith accretion: five historical event systems later needing a \"unifying projection\" (`events/feed.py` admits this), a 6,785-line orchestrator.py, and parallel hash-chain implementations (journal, WAL, EventJournal, work ledger, lineage, audit chain) that only partially share one contract — plus `pickle` for local caches where JSON would do.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-137-- **Scores:** determinism 9, durability 9, security 8, recovery 9, auditability 10, modularity 6, testability 9 (Hypothesis stateful WAL-recovery machine, lineage tamper-detection integration tests, mutation testing wired for lineage — VERIFIED test tree), windows 8, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 9 (as pattern source; ~2 as dependency)\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-138-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-139-### orca — Workflow-as-code agent orchestration: typed, resumable Scala flows where deterministic structure (stages+git) owns nondeterministic agent operations\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-140-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-141-- **Identity:** https://github.com/VirtusLab/orca @ 9da3d07 (2026-08-09) | Apache-2.0, standard text, no riders (VERIFIED — read head+tail of LICENSE) | Scala 3.8.4 (~75k LOC, 449 files) + POSIX sh | sbt (multi-module: tools/flow/runner/shell + 5 backend drivers); flows are `scala-cli` `.sc` scripts, JVM 21 | ~1.4 MiB pack\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-142-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — no telemetry found, MCP/HTTP listeners are loopback-only (`McpHost.scala`: Netty on `127.0.0.1`, ephemeral port, VERIFIED); but by design it runs repo-committed lint/format commands via `bash -c` (`Lint.scala:33`, settings from `.orca/settings.properties`), performs `git reset --hard` + `git clean -fdq` in failure teardown (`GitTool.scala:646-655`), spawns vendor agent CLIs as subprocesses, and `install.sh` is curl|bash that bootstraps scala-cli remotely and ships a shim resolving unpinned `latest.release` at every launch (all VERIFIED). Fine as read-only source; unaudited execution not advised.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-143-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — orca is the strongest workflow-as-code specimen in the registry for GNOSIS's exact thesis: a deterministic host program (typed stages, git commits, bounded loops) owns state and gates while LLM agents only propose within them. But it is Scala/JVM with an explicit \"Windows beyond the bash -c contract is a non-goal\" (`docs/research/shell/06-wizard-settings.md`, VERIFIED), and its capability enforcement leans on Scala 3 capture checking — untransplantable as a dependency. Reimplement its stage/progress-log/recovery patterns in the GNOSIS Python kernel.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-144-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:145:  - Resumable checkpointed stages (`flow/src/main/scala/orca/Flow.scala:stage`, `StageFrames.scala`): `stage(name){body}` gets a hierarchical id `parent/name#occurrence` from a frame stack of per-parent occurrence counters; a fresh run serializes the body's typed result JSON into the progress log and makes ONE git commit covering code+log; on re-run, a log entry that decodes to `T` replays the result and skips the body (decode failure = fail-safe re-run). Invariants: exactly-once occurrence bump per attempt (stable ids across resumes); skipped parents structurally can't desync nested counters; owner-thread assert rejects `stage()` from forks. Failure mode: outer stage re-running over a nested stage's committed partial work requires idempotent bodies (documented verbatim in scaladoc). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-146-  - Durable progress log + untrusted-on-load validation (`flow/.../progress/ProgressStore.scala`, `RecoveryCheck.scala`): prompt-hash-keyed JSON file written via temp-file + atomic rename (torn-write proof), tri-state load (`Absent`/`Corrupt`/`Loaded`) so corrupt≠absent gets a loud warning; on resume the header is treated as hostile input — branch-ref shape checks blocking CLI-flag injection/path traversal (`isSafeReusedRef`), protected-branch floor (`main`/`master` + detected default), promptHash recompute, and a current-branch cross-check — all before any destructive git action; invalid = hard abort, never silent fresh start. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-147-  - Lifecycle recovery with disjoint teardowns (`runner/.../FlowLifecycle.scala`): setup refuses to start a fresh run on a branch another run's log already claims (`abortIfBranchBusy`); failure teardown `reset --hard` (+ gated clean) back to the last stage commit so re-run resumes in place; success teardown removes the log in a final commit and auto-deletes only branches orca itself minted (`BranchMode.Created` gate defeats tampered-header-driven deletion); deterministic `flow-<hash>` fallback branch names so unattended runs never flip on naming collisions. Plus dual locks (`FlowLock.scala`): process AtomicBoolean + PID lockfile with CREATE_NEW-only atomicity, stale-lock steal by delete+re-race, bounded at 4 attempts. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-148-  - Capability-token effect discipline (`flow/.../FlowControl.scala`, `InStage`/`WorkspaceWrite` tokens, `Session.scala:OutsideStage`): who may start stages (`FlowControl`), mutate the workspace (`WorkspaceWrite`), or mint durable sessions (compile-time proof of being *outside* a stage via `NotGiven[InStage]`) is encoded in types; forks receive only the narrow read+emit `FlowContext`, enforced by Scala capture/separation checking in `ReviewLoop.scala` plus a runtime owner-thread assert as backstop — \"LLM fan-out can read, never commit\" is a compile error, not a convention. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-149-  - Bounded review-and-fix loop with a nothing-is-lost ledger (`flow/.../review/ReviewLoop.scala`, `ConfidenceGate.scala`, `GateLedger.scala`): parallel reviewers with durable per-reviewer sessions re-reviewing only the delta since `lastSent`; per-severity confidence bars (tentative Critical admitted at 0.5, uncertain Info gated at 0.8); hard caps (3 fix attempts) and a halt when the fixer reports zero fixes; every finding gated out, declined, or unaccounted-for is returned in `IgnoredIssues` with the last-known reason and printed at exit — the loop cannot silently drop evidence. Fixer's declines (not its \"fixed\" claims) are fed to next-round reviewers, so reviewers re-derive fixes independently. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-150-  - Enforcement honesty matrix (`tools/.../agents/Enforcement.scala` + `EnforcementTableTest`): every backend's actual enforcement of a tool restriction is classified `Hard`/`SandboxApprox`/`PromptOnly`/`Ignored` with per-cell rationale, machine-checked in tests — the framework refuses to pretend a prompt-level \"read-only\" is a sandbox. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-151-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:152:  - **Stage = typed checkpoint = one git commit.** Persist each stage's typed result JSON in a run-scoped progress log, commit code+log atomically, and resume by replaying decoded results with decode-failure→re-run as the fail-safe; use hierarchical `name#occurrence` ids minted by a frame stack so same-named and nested stages stay stable across resumes.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-153-  - **Treat your own recovery state as hostile input.** Validate resume headers (ref-shape/injection checks, protected-branch floor, prompt-hash, current-branch cross-check) before any destructive operation; make invalid state a hard abort, distinguish corrupt-log from absent-log, and gate branch deletion on \"did I mint this branch\" recorded at creation time.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-154-  - **Nothing-lost gate ledger for review loops.** Every finding a gate holds back or a fixer declines is carried, deduped by title with the freshest reason, and returned+printed at every loop exit — maps directly onto GNOSIS's EVIDENCE BEFORE CONSENSUS and incident→test rules.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-155-  - **Enforcement matrix as first-class policy metadata.** For each adapter (Claude/Codex), record whether a permission restriction is mechanically enforced, sandbox-approximated, prompt-only, or ignored — and machine-check the table in tests, feeding the Kernel's least-privilege decisions with honest data.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-156-- **What to avoid:** (1) `bash -c` execution of commands read from a repo-committed settings file that an agent itself auto-discovers and writes — a prompt-injected agent can plant a shell command a later run executes; GNOSIS's Policy Engine must admission-check any repo-sourced command. (2) The unpinned `latest.release` launcher shim (every launch resolves the newest published artifact) — violates GNOSIS reproducibility; pin versions in provenance. Also note the documented resume hazard: outer-stage bodies must be idempotent over their own committed leftovers — design stages so edits never straddle a nested stage.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-157-- **Scores:** determinism 8, durability 8, security 7, recovery 9, auditability 8, modularity 8, testability 9, windows 3, op-simplicity 5, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-158-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-159-### agent-workspace-fabric — control plane that runs AI coding agents through isolated worktree→validate→PR→monitor→merge lifecycles\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-160-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-161-- **Identity:** https://github.com/dimileeh/agent-workspace-fabric @ 45693fe | Apache-2.0, no riders (standard text verbatim, appendix unfilled) [VERIFIED] | Python 3.12 (FastAPI, SQLAlchemy 2 async, Pydantic v2, Alembic, Docker SDK) + TypeScript (Next.js console in `apps/console`) | uv/pyproject + npm | ~41 MB w/o `.git`, 484 src py files, 1030 test files, coverage gate `fail_under = 99` in pyproject [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-162-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — no telemetry, no eval/exec, only SafeLoader-subclass YAML, secrets redaction + egress audit modules present [VERIFIED]; but the control-plane containers run as root with CAP_SYS_ADMIN for overlayfs auth mounts (`docker/compose/local-service.yml:198`, `src/awf/node/auth_mounts_caps.py`), opt-in `privileged: true` DinD sidecar (`node/compose_manager.py:1131`), and it shells out to agent CLIs and git/docker pervasively (by design). `git push --force-with-lease` appears only in rebase recovery on AWF's own feature branch (`executor/git_methods.py:989`); worktree cleanup deliberately avoids blanket `reset --hard` (`executor/git_ops.py:337`) [VERIFIED]. Nothing runs at import/install time; pre-commit hooks are local lint/type checks [VERIFIED].\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-163-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — this is the closest existing system to GNOSIS's task→worktree→isolated-run→validate→PR→integrate loop, and its three or four core patterns (immutable transition allow-list, pure no-I/O decision core, DB leases with fencing epochs, reason-codes flowing end-to-end into an append-only event log) map directly onto \"kernel owns state/gates, agents propose, evidence before consensus\". As a dependency it is rejected: Postgres + per-workspace Compose stacks + root/CAP_SYS_ADMIN worker + GitHub-as-source-of-truth is far too heavy and Linux-container-bound for a Windows-native SQLite-first kernel. Re-implement the mechanisms; benchmark GNOSIS's lifecycle vocabulary against its state diagram.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-164-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-165-  - Workspace state machine (`src/awf/control/state_machine.py:WorkspaceStateMachine`): a module-level `MappingProxyType` of `frozenset` allowed transitions (`requested→provisioning→ready→running→validating→pushing→monitoring_pr→completed`, plus non-terminal `blocked`/`recovering` pause states and `destroying→destroyed`); every status mutation must call `assert_transition(src, dst)` which raises a typed error carrying both endpoints for structured 409s. Invariant: no illegal transition can be silently written; terminal/callback-terminal sets gate cleanup and stale async callbacks. Failure mode: the guard is a convention (explicit call, not enforced by type system) — a forgotten call bypasses it. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-166-  - Pure PR-monitor decision core (`src/awf/runtime/pr_monitor.py:decide`): zero-I/O function `(PRStatus, MonitorState, MonitorConfig) → exactly one MonitorAction` over an explicitly ordered 10-gate table (merged/closed short-circuit → base-retarget drift → SyncBase-before-comments → operator hint → unresolved comments → blocking reviews → CI fail → CI pending → conflict → absent-required-checks grace → blocked/hooks → Merge-or-NotifyHuman when `auto_merge=False`); the runner (`runtime/pr_monitor_runner/runner.py`) does all I/O and persists `MonitorState`. Invariant: one action per call, gate order documented with the production incidents that forced it; no iteration/wall-clock terminal gate — exit only via Merge/ShortCircuit/Abort. Failure mode: livelock is possible by design and delegated to human notification gates. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-167-  - DB-backed claims with fencing epochs (`src/awf/control/worker/manager.py:ControlWorker`, `worker/claims.py:_apply_execution_claim`): worker polls Postgres with `SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, claims rows via conditional status CAS, stamps `execution_claimed_by` + expiry + `execution_claim_epoch = epoch + 1` as a SQL expression so a later claimant always lands a strictly higher epoch and a stale worker's heartbeat/release CAS is fenced. Invariant: at most one live driver per workspace across concurrent workers/restarts. Failure mode: epoch fencing covers only the provisioning path (executor path documented as epoch-unguarded). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-168-  - Mirror + worktree provisioner (`src/awf/node/git_manager.py:GitManager`): per-repo bare mirror (`clone --mirror`, then strips `remote.origin.mirror` so worktrees can push), per-workspace `git worktree add -b <branch> <tracking_ref>`; all mirror-registry mutations serialized by per-mirror asyncio locks keyed by event loop (worktree registry is a shared file that corrupts under concurrent add/prune — observed in their CI); removal is idempotent + `worktree prune`, and `_reclaim_stale_worktree` raises a reason-coded error on OSError rather than silently leaking disk. Invariant: base lookups go through remote-tracking refs so prune never touches AWF-created branches. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-169-  - Stale-execution recovery & GC (`src/awf/control/worker/recovery_stale.py`, `src/awf/service/gc*.py` — 15 modules): periodic scans classify runtime snapshots of stranded active workspaces and choose salvage (preserved-active resume, hosted PR re-adoption, remonitor of an open PR with a running stack) before failing; state machine carries dedicated salvage edges (e.g. `provisioning→monitoring_pr`, `validating→running` rewind). Invariant: cleanup is honest (failures recorded as events, never swallowed); orphan dirs reconciled against DB. [VERIFIED at structure level; individual salvage paths NOT TESTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-170-  - Reason codes + append-only evidence (`src/awf/db/models.py:WorkspaceEvent`, `common/reason_codes`-generated `docs/REASON_CATALOG.md` ~936 lines): exception `reason_code` → structured log → append-only `WorkspaceEvent` (per-workspace `event_sequence` counter, \"no UPDATE/DELETE ever\") → `FailureReason` → policy; `idempotency_key` unique constraints on workspaces/tasks/operations/callbacks; bounded validate/fix loop with explicit pass budgets (`executor/execution_validation.py: max_validation_attempts = max_fix_passes + conformance_budget + 1`) and flaky/setup retry budgets in `runtime/validation_runner.py`. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-171-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-172-  - The state machine as an importable, frozen allow-list module with `assert_transition` raising a typed error carrying `(from, to, allowed_next)` — plus documented salvage edges for restart recovery — as the Kernel's single status authority.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:173:  - The pure-`decide()` pattern for any monitor/review loop: snapshot + persisted state in, exactly one action out, all I/O in a thin runner — this makes GNOSIS gate policy unit-testable and replayable with hand-rolled snapshots.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-174-  - Claim leases with monotonic fencing epochs bumped as a DB expression (`epoch = epoch + 1` in the claim txn), so a superseded agent/worker is fenced on its next CAS — directly portable to SQLite.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-175-  - Reason-code plumbing end-to-end (exception → log field → append-only event → failure taxonomy → scheduler policy, e.g. `RETRY_BONUS_INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE` in `service/scheduler.py`) with a generated catalog doc as the drift gate — matches GNOSIS rules 5–7 exactly.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-176-- **What to avoid:** the `*DelegatesMixin` file-explosion style (~45 executor modules of free functions taking `self: Any`) trades away static typing and makes control flow archaeology hard; and the guard-by-convention state machine (nothing structurally prevents `workspace.status = ...` — GNOSIS should make the kernel the only writer of status, not a convention). Also avoid the root+CAP_SYS_ADMIN control-plane posture; keep GNOSIS least-privilege.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-177-- **Scores:** determinism 8, durability 9, security 6, recovery 9, auditability 9, modularity 7, testability 9, windows 3, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-178-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-179-### cezar — local multi-runner (Claude/Codex/OpenCode/pi) coding-agent orchestrator with per-task worktrees, crash re-adoption and usage-limit auto-resume\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-180-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-181-- **Identity:** https://github.com/open-mercato/cezar @ 9a8b24d (shallow clone, depth 1; HEAD 2026-08-19) | MIT, standard 21-line text, no riders (VERIFIED) | TypeScript on Node >= 20, npm workspaces, plain `tsc` build, Hono HTTP server; deps: hono, zod, ws, yaml, smol-toml, @clack/prompts | ~110k LoC TS across 4 packages, 17 MiB pack, 249 test files vs 256 source files\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-182-- **Risk:** LOW (as read-only pattern source) — no install hooks (root and package `package.json` have no pre/postinstall; prebuild/postbuild are local build scripts) [VERIFIED]; server binds 127.0.0.1 by default with an anchored host-guard against DNS rebinding (`server/server.ts:~1224-1260`) [VERIFIED]; secret redaction before persisting transcripts (`core/secret-redaction.ts`, value+pattern, shared regex with child-env stripping) [VERIFIED]; destructive git ops scoped to `.ai/cezar/worktrees/<runId>` [VERIFIED]. Caveats if ever *run*: the zero-config tool default grants unrestricted `Bash` in cwd (self-documented as #430 in `agent-runner.ts`) and autosaves commit with `--no-verify`; `server-install` ships VPS/ngrok exposure scripts (opt-in, not audited in depth) [VERIFIED/NOT TESTED].\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-183-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — This is the closest production-grade match yet to GNOSIS's priority problem (one seam over Claude Code and Codex CLIs with resume, worktrees, and gates), but it is a Node/TS application with a 3.6k-line manager class, not a library GNOSIS (Python/SQLite kernel) can depend on. Its mechanisms map one-to-one onto GNOSIS rules — `RATE_LIMITED != FAIL_CODE` is literally implemented as evidence-ordered usage-limit parsing with durable resume deadlines, and the review park is a kernel-owned gate the agent cannot bypass. Reimplement the seam, the classification, and the recovery sweep in the GNOSIS kernel; benchmark nothing, depend on nothing.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-184-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-185-  - Runner abstraction (`packages/cezar/src/core/agent-runner.ts:AgentRunner/AgentSession/RUNNER_IDS`): one normalized `AgentEvent` stream + a persistent-process session (sendMessage/end/interrupt/result) implemented by 4 backends — claude = stream-json over stdin, codex = `codex app-server` JSON-RPC/JSONL (`thread/start`, `thread/resume`, `turn/steer`, `turn/interrupt`), opencode = HTTP+SSE, pi = JSONL RPC. `RUNNER_IDS` tuple is the single source of truth all schemas derive from; per-backend system-prompt mapping is explicit (claude `--append-system-prompt`, others prepend to first user message). Invariant: an exit cezar caused is never an agent failure — `isSignalTerminationExit(130/137/143)` paired with a \"we sent the signal\" flag, and `trackChildExit` deliberately avoids the `ChildProcess.killed` trap (#844). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-186-  - Failure classification + auto-resume (`core/usage-limit.ts:parseUsageLimit`, `workflows/run.ts:scheduleAutoResumeIfLimited/fireAutoResume/reconcileAutoResumes`): terminal error strings are classified in strict evidence order — Claude's machine marker `usage limit reached|<epoch>`, ISO timestamp in prose, wall-clock+timezone, relative delay — and each hit records which shape carried it (`evidence` field, quoted in the transcript). No reset instant ⇒ `null`, deliberately refusing to guess. Resume deadline is durable on the record (`autoResumeAt`); timers are process-local and rebuilt by a reconcile sweep on every pump, bounded by `MAX_AUTO_RESUMES=12`, a 24 h missed-deadline expiry, and a 30 s grace past the provider's reset. Account-scoped holds (`runner:profile` key) distinguish deadline holds (block everything — prevents resume stampede) from in-flight holds (block only new work — prevents resume deadlock). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-187-  - Crash recovery (`workflows/run.ts:recover()/reviveQueuedRun`, `handoff.ts`): on boot, `queued` runs are re-queued from the persisted `workflowDef` (or failed loudly — \"a run neither running nor failed is the worst failure this engine has\"); `waiting` runs settle like a closed session; `running` runs are marked `failed: interrupted` then immediately resumed via the last persisted `sessionId` with a prompt pointing the agent at its per-run handoff file. The engine itself heartbeats that handoff journal, so resume context exists even when the agent never wrote. A 60 s queue watchdog re-adopts records the live process lost. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-188-  - Worktree lifecycle (`git-worktree.ts:createWorktree/autosaveCommit/resolveBaseRef`; `workflows/run.ts:acquireRepoRoot`): per-run branch `cez/<id8>` under `.ai/cezar/worktrees/<runId>`; creation is idempotent with reattach paths and never deletes non-empty unregistered dirs (recoverable work). Autosave commits carry a reason tag (`periodic|turn end|run finalize|pre-PR`) and *refuse* to commit mid-merge or conflict-marker states (ordered `<<<<<<</=======/>>>>>>>` triple scan) — losing a recovery point beats poisoning the branch. Stale-base resolution prefers `origin/<base>` when local is behind, killing phantom inflated diffs. Runs in the repo root are serialized by a promise-chain lease held for the run's whole lifetime, whose waiters park and return their concurrency slot. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-189-  - Review gate (`runs/review-gate.ts:reviewGateEnabled` + `run.ts:settleSuccess`): a successful run parks at status `review` (blocking retention/cleanup) only when `worktreeHasDiff && gateEnabled && !autonomous` — a state the store owns, not a prompt convention; done-signals from agents are line-anchored in-band markers (`CEZ:DONE`, `CEZ:PR=`) parsed from the agent's own turn text only, never from tool output, so reading the contract cannot poison the record (`runs/task-markers.ts`). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-190-  - Durable store (`runs/store.ts`): `runs.json` index written atomic tmp+rename, per-run NDJSON event log via append; `reconcileLoadedRun` demotes impossible statuses at load time (a `running` record loaded without `keepLive` becomes `failed`). Plain files, no DB. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-191-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-192-  1. The two-hold account model for rate limits: a *deadline* hold (window known shut — blocks all work on that `runner:account` key, including other resumes) vs an *in-flight* hold (one resume is probing — blocks new work only). Each hold kind fixed a real bug in opposite directions; GNOSIS's Kernel should key limits by backend+credential, exactly this way.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-193-  2. Evidence-graded limit parsing that refuses to guess: ordered recognizers (machine marker > timestamp > clock > delay), a recorded `evidence` tag on every classification, `null` when no reset instant — surfaced honestly instead of retry-looping. Slots directly under GNOSIS's `RATE_LIMITED` taxonomy entry.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-194-  3. Recovery as reconcile, not restore: durable deadline/state on the record, volatile timers rebuilt idempotently on every scheduler pump plus a wedge watchdog, and boot re-adoption that either revives a run through the normal queue path or fails it loudly — never leaves a ghost.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-195-  4. Engine-side handoff journal per task (seeded idempotently, heartbeated by the kernel, injected via env var into every resume prompt) — resume context that survives the agent, the process, and the provider window.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-196-  5. Autosave that refuses conflict states and tags its reason — provenance-grade recovery commits without poisoning the branch.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-197-- **What to avoid:** (1) `RunManager` is a 3,645-line class owning queue, leases, resume, review, retention and prompt composition — GNOSIS must keep these as separate kernel modules with typed interfaces. (2) Failure classification exists *only* as regex over provider prose at the terminal error; GNOSIS should type failures at the adapter boundary (structured exit/frame codes first) and use prose parsing solely as a last-resort enricher — and never copy the default-unrestricted-Bash tool allowlist.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-198-- **Scores:** determinism 6, durability 8, security 6, recovery 9, auditability 8, modularity 7, testability 9, windows 7, op-simplicity 8, unique-value 8, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-199-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-200-### ralphex — autonomous plan-execution loop (task → multi-agent review → external adversarial review → finalize) driving Claude/Codex CLIs\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-201-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-202-- **Identity:** https://github.com/umputun/ralphex @ 935cc87 | MIT, standard text, no riders (VERIFIED) | Go 1.26 (+POSIX shell/Python wrapper scripts) | Makefile + go build, vendored deps (26MB) | ~17.3k LOC source Go, ~36.2k LOC tests, shallow clone (1 commit)\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-203-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — code itself is clean (no telemetry, dashboard binds 127.0.0.1 by default `pkg/web/server.go:41`, strips `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`/`CLAUDECODE` from child env `claudeChildEnv`, no `reset --hard`/`clean`/push, only `worktree remove --force` on its own worktrees — all VERIFIED), but its operating mode is intrinsically privileged: embedded default `claude_args = --dangerously-skip-permissions` (VERIFIED `pkg/config/defaults/config:23`) plus config-driven arbitrary script execution (`custom_review_script`, `vcs_command`, custom notify script). Docker-only opt-in `npm install @latest` at container start is documented, not in the binary.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-204-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the convergence/stop-condition set (dual-evidence completion, tri-state review signal contract, git-fingerprint stalemate counter, three-tier retry/limit/error taxonomy) is the best worked-out implementation-review-rework loop seen so far and maps almost one-to-one onto GNOSIS rules 5–9/11. But ralphex is a prompt-governed CLI orchestrator where the LLM self-reports state via text markers, not a kernel that owns state and gates; GNOSIS should reimplement these conditions as kernel-enforced gates over durable evidence, not depend on or embed the tool.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-205-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-206-  - Task-loop dual-evidence completion (`pkg/processor/phase/task.go:TaskPhase.Run` + `HasUncompletedTasks`): loop `i=1..MaxIterations` (default 50); `ALL_TASKS_DONE` signal alone is NOT trusted — the plan file is re-parsed and if unchecked `[ ]` boxes remain the signal is discarded with a warning and the loop continues. `FAILED` signal gets bounded retries (`retryCount`, default 1) then hard error; timeout → fixed 5s `retryBackoff` re-run; exhaustion → hard error \"max iterations reached\". Invariant: completion = signal ∧ durable-artifact evidence. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-207-  - Tri-state review round contract (`pkg/config/defaults/prompts/review_first.txt` / `review_second.txt` + `pkg/processor/phase/review.go:ReviewPhase.Loop`): Path A `REVIEW_DONE` = \"this iteration found ZERO issues\" (explicitly not \"I finished fixing\"); Path B fixed-and-committed = emit NO marker → loop schedules another verification round; Path C can't-fix = `TASK_FAILED`. Loop bound `max(3, MaxIterations/10)`; also exits when git HEAD is unchanged after a round (fixed-point detection). Failure mode: max-iterations exit is soft (\"continuing...\") — unconverged review flows on. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-208-  - Stalemate detection (`pkg/processor/phase/git_state.go:stalemateState.Update`): before each Claude-eval round a `gitSnapshot{HeadHash, DiffFingerprint}` is taken; N consecutive rounds with identical snapshot (`ReviewPatience`, 0=disabled) terminates the external review early; counter resets on any change; refuses to count when head/diff evidence is missing. Invariant: no progress evidence for N rounds ⇒ STALEMATE, stop burning tokens. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-209-  - Adversarial external review loop (`pkg/processor/phase/external_review.go:runLoop`/`runIteration`): independent tool (codex read-only, or custom script) reviews → Claude verifies each finding against code and fixes or rebuts → rebuttals fed back to the external tool next round (`{{PREVIOUS_REVIEW_CONTEXT}}`). Stop set: `CODEX_REVIEW_DONE` (only legal when the external tool itself reported no issues, per `codex.txt` contract), empty external output → break, stalemate, or bound `max(3, MaxIterations/5)` → soft continue. Cross-model disagreement is thus bounded by both patience and iterations. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-210-  - Three-tier output-pattern failure taxonomy (`pkg/processor/execution_policy.go:retryPolicy.Run` + `pkg/executor/executor.go:matchPattern`): retry patterns (transient 529/502/503/504) → treated as timeout, 5s backoff, no wait gate; limit patterns → wait `wait_on_limit` then retry, else surface as error; error patterns → hard exit with help command. Retry tier is suppressed when a structured signal is present so a completed run isn't discarded. Mirrors GNOSIS `RATE_LIMITED != FAIL_CODE`. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-211-  - Code-injected reviewer contract (`pkg/processor/prompts.go:reviewContextInstruction` + 5 specialized agents in `pkg/config/defaults/agents/`): every spawned reviewer (quality/implementation/testing/simplification/documentation, launched in parallel) gets a Go-side prepended contract — read-only (\"the parent loop owns fixing\"), no sub-delegation, evidence threshold, diff scope, `NO ISSUES FOUND` sentinel — that user prompt files cannot drop. Reviewer-judges/parent-fixes separation enforced at orchestrator level. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-212-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-213-  - **Signal ∧ evidence completion gate**: never let an agent's DONE claim close a task; the kernel re-checks a durable artifact (checkbox state, git HEAD/diff fingerprint) and downgrades signal-without-evidence to \"continue with warning\".\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-214-  - **Tri-state round protocol where silence means rework**: DONE only for a zero-findings round; \"I fixed things\" must trigger another independent verification round; convergence is declared only by a clean round, never by the fixer.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-215-  - **Stalemate counter keyed on repo-state fingerprints** (HEAD + diff hash), N consecutive unchanged rounds → `STALEMATE` outcome; reset on change; never count when evidence collection failed — implement as a kernel circuit breaker.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-216-  - **Tiered pattern taxonomy with signal-suppressed retries**: retry/limit/error classes, limit-class gated on an explicit wait budget, and transient markers ignored when a structured completion signal exists.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-217-- **What to avoid:** (1) In-band substring signal detection — `detectSignal` matches `<<<RALPHEX:...>>>` anywhere in output, so a model *quoting* the marker triggers it; ralphex defends only with prompt exhortations (\"a marker is irreversible\"). GNOSIS must use out-of-band structured results. (2) Fail-open loop exhaustion — both review loops log \"max iterations reached, continuing...\" and proceed to finalize as if successful, and \"HEAD unchanged\" is read as convergence even when the reviewer may simply have failed to act; GNOSIS gates must fail closed and distinguish \"no findings\" from \"no action\".\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-218-- **Scores:** determinism 6, durability 6, security 6, recovery 7, auditability 7, modularity 8, testability 9, windows 6, op-simplicity 8, unique-value 8, evidence-quality 8, gnosis-fit 8\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-219-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-220-### smithers — durable time-travel runtime/control plane for long-running coding-agent workflows (JSX workflows over SQLite/Postgres)\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-221-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-222-- **Identity:** https://github.com/smithersai/smithers @ 584f479 (tag v0.35.0, 2026-08-17) | MIT, plain text, no riders (vendored `@smthrs/jj-*` binary packages carry Apache-2.0 for the Jujutsu binary) | JavaScript ESM w/ JSDoc types + type-only `.ts` sidecars, React/JSX, Effect-TS 4 beta | pnpm workspaces + Bun (Node >=22, Bun >=1.3), tsup dts-only | ~9,515 tracked files, 69 MiB pack, ~50 workspace packages\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-223-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — no remote telemetry found (searched posthog/segment/amplitude/sentry: only local Prometheus/OTel + opt-in stderr JSON [VERIFIED]); postinstall hooks are chmod-only on vendored jj binaries [VERIFIED]; gateway enforces loopback Host allowlist + auth token with explicit DNS-rebinding defense (`packages/server/src/serve.js`) [VERIFIED]; the MEDIUM comes from a very large supply chain (pinned `effect@4.0.0-beta.105`, electrobun, react-reconciler), release tooling that downloads jj from GitHub releases (`scripts/fetch-jj-binaries.mjs`) [VERIFIED], and its core purpose of spawning agent CLIs/sandboxes (Docker/Bubblewrap/Microsandbox) [VERIFIED design, NOT TESTED].\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:224:- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the mechanism designs (frame log, write-ahead tool journal, effect-boundary classification, leased/fenced destructive rewind) are exactly what a deterministic kernel that owns state and gates needs, and they are the most rigorous articulation of agent time travel seen in this survey. But the substrate (Bun + JSX re-render via react-reconciler + Effect-TS beta across ~50 packages) contradicts GNOSIS's Python/SQLite minimal-kernel constitution and would be an unauditable dependency. Steal the schemas and invariants; do not adopt the runtime.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-225-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-226-  - Frame log + content-addressed snapshots (`packages/db/src/frame-codec.js`, `packages/engine/src/engine.js:persistDriverFrame` ~L10716, `packages/time-travel/src/snapshot/captureSnapshotEffect.js`): every workflow re-render appends a row to `_smithers_frames` — every 50th a full keyframe, the rest versioned JSON-path delta ops, so reconstructing any frame is bounded to <50 applies; each frame's full state (nodes, outputs, loop state, input, plus a signal-provenance horizon) is stored as a sha256 content-addressed, refcounted snapshot row *in the same transaction as the frame insert*. Invariant: any frame is reconstructible and the snapshot, not heuristics, is the rewind source of truth. Failure mode: legacy runs without snapshots fall back to timestamp-cutoff heuristics (explicitly logged). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:227:  - Pure decision core (`packages/scheduler/src/scheduleTasks.js`, `makeWorkflowSession.js`): scheduler has no IO/DB/timers — a pure function from plan tree + task-state map (keys `nodeId::iteration`, loop scopes suffixed `@@ralphId=iteration`) to the next decision; code comments state and enforce that a resumed run replays identical activation decisions from a restored state map in descriptor order. Saga and try-catch-finally regions match failures by error code. Invariant: resume determinism = f(persisted state), engine is just the effectful shell. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-228-  - Write-ahead tool journal + effect boundary (`packages/engine/src/createToolJournalContext.js`, `packages/time-travel/src/guardEffectBoundary.js`, `assessEffectBoundary.js`): every tool/task call inserts a journal row with status `intended` and provenance flags (`sideEffect`, `idempotent`, `hasRevert`, `idempotencyKey`) *before* execution, then completes it. Rewind/fork classifies journaled effects crossing the boundary as blocking / revertible / warning; revertible effects require a resolvable compensation handler from the *identical* workflow source — `validateWorkflowIdentity.js` sha256-hashes the whole transitively-imported module graph because \"compensation handlers must never be guessed\"; blocked crossings fail unless `force:true`, and forced crossings are durably recorded. Forks never revert parent effects (warnings only). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-229-  - Destructive rewind as leased, fenced, single-transaction mutation (`packages/time-travel/src/jumpToFrame.js`, 1,690 lines): requires `confirm:true`; per-run durable rewind lease with renew/`checkStillHeld` re-checked *inside* the DB transaction as a fence; rate limit; an `in_progress` audit row written before any mutation so `recoverRewindAuditsAtStartup` can recover a mid-rewind crash; jj (Jujutsu) working-copy pointer reverts per sandbox cwd with a rollback plan; frames/snapshots/vcs-tags/attempts/outputs/diff-cache truncation and the `TimeTravelJumped` event commit atomically; post-commit failures explicitly never undo the committed rewind; failures mark the run `needs_attention`. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:230:  - Ownership, liveness and resume gating (`packages/db/src/runtime-owner.js`, `packages/engine/src/engine.js` ~L3030): runs carry `runtimeOwnerId` = `pid:<pid>@<host>:<session>` + heartbeat; liveness = local PID probe or heartbeat freshness (remote owners never PID-probed); rewind/fork refuse live-owner runs without `force`. Resume refuses on entry-hash / module-graph-hash / VCS-root mismatch (`RESUME_METADATA_MISMATCH`) unless the human re-blesses with `--accept-workflow-change` (\"you own replay determinism from that point\"). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-231-  - Durable pause states (`packages/engine/src/approvals.js`, `run-parking.js`, `runsDueForQuotaResume`): approvals are DB rows resolved under a node lock, with approval admissions restored across resume; host shutdown parks runs as durable `paused`; rate-limit exhaustion parks as `waiting-quota` with a `resetAtMs` sweep — quota is a park state, not a failure. [VERIFIED, approvals partially read]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:232:- **What GNOSIS should steal:** (1) The write-ahead tool journal: persist `intended` + {sideEffect, idempotent, hasRevert, idempotencyKey} before any tool executes, making rewind/fork safety a *decidable query* over the journal instead of a guess — this slots directly into GNOSIS's evidence-before-consensus kernel. (2) Frame log = keyframe-every-N + JSON-delta rows plus content-addressed refcounted snapshots in one SQLite transaction — full run history and O(1)-bounded reconstruction at trivial cost. (3) Workflow-identity hash (entry + full import-graph sha256) pinned at run start, with resume/compensation gated on exact match and an explicit human re-bless flag — a clean Task!=session determinism contract. (4) Destructive operations as leased+fenced single transactions with a pre-mutation `in_progress` audit row and startup crash recovery; and `RATE_LIMITED` modeled as a durable park state with a reset timestamp.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-233-- **What to avoid:** The 12,000-line `engine.js` and `\"./*\"` wildcard exports that make every file in ~50 packages public API — the operational surface is enormous and filenames become unbreakable contracts. Deriving scheduler input by re-rendering JSX through a react-reconciler on a pinned *beta* dependency (`effect@4.0.0-beta.105`, Bun.Transpiler with regex fallback) puts a large nondeterminism/supply-chain surface under the determinism-critical path; GNOSIS's graph derivation must stay a small, boring pure function.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-234-- **Scores:** determinism 7, durability 9, security 7, recovery 9, auditability 9, modularity 7, testability 8, windows 6, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 8, gnosis-fit 7\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-235-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-236-### beads — dependency-aware distributed issue/task graph for AI agents (\"bd\"), Dolt-backed work queue with atomic claims and lease-based crash recovery\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-237-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-238-- **Identity:** https://github.com/gastownhall/beads @ 309ebd4 (2026-08-19) | MIT, no riders (LICENSE read in full — standard MIT text, \"Copyright (c) 2025 Beads Contributors\") [VERIFIED] | Go 1.26.5 (~341K non-test LOC + ~456K test LOC), plus small Python MCP server (fastmcp) and npm/PS1/winget installers | Go modules + Makefile + Nix flake | 53MB working tree, 3,567 tracked files\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-239-- **Risk:** LOW — for read-only pattern mining: no eval/unsafe deserialization; SQL is parameterized with hardcoded table names; telemetry is explicit opt-in (`BD_OTEL_ENABLED=true`, defaults to localhost OTLP) [VERIFIED]; Anthropic API used only for opt-in `bd compact` summarization and errors without an explicit key rather than falling back silently [VERIFIED]; Dolt sql-server listener binds 127.0.0.1 by default [VERIFIED]. If ever *run* as a tool, risk rises to MEDIUM: npm postinstall downloads release binaries from GitHub [VERIFIED], `bd init`/`bd setup claude|codex` write agent hooks/AGENTS.md into the host project [SELF-REPORTED, install code not fully read], and it spawns a local MySQL-protocol server process [VERIFIED].\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-240-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — Beads solves exactly GNOSIS's persistent-task-graph problem, but as its own kernel: a Go binary owning state in Dolt (embedded via cgo, or an external `dolt sql-server` in non-cgo builds), with its own claim semantics, migrations (60+), proxy daemon, and doctor. Adopting it as a dependency would place Task state under an external runtime outside the GNOSIS Kernel/Policy Engine (violating \"Kernel authorizes states\") and import Dolt's operational weight onto a Windows workstation where SQLite-V1 is the ADR'd baseline. The mechanisms, however, are battle-tested (code comments cite specific regression IDs for every subtle choice) and map 1:1 onto SQLite reimplementation. Keep it as a secondary BENCHMARK reference for ready/claim semantics once GNOSIS's scheduler exists.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-241-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-242-  - Task/dependency schema (`internal/storage/schema/migrations/0001,0002*.sql`; `internal/types/types.go:DependencyType`): one `issues` table (status, priority, assignee, defer_until, ephemeral, await_type/await_id for gates) + one typed edge table `(issue_id, depends_on_id, type, metadata JSON)` PK'd on the pair. 19 well-known edge types but only 4 affect scheduling (`AffectsReadyWork()`: blocks, parent-child, conditional-blocks, waits-for); association/provenance edges (related, discovered-from, caused-by, authored-by…) ride the same table without touching readiness. `IsSchedulingEdge()` is the single shared cycle-set predicate, deliberately excluding waits-for because its gate clears on children, not the target, so it cannot close a cycle. Invariant: scheduler semantics live in exactly one function each. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-243-  - Ready detection via materialized `is_blocked` (`internal/storage/sqlbuild/ready.go:BuildReadyWorkWhere`; `internal/storage/issueops/blocked_state.go:markBlockedTemplateForIssues`): \"ready\" is a cheap indexed query — active status AND `is_blocked = 0` AND not pinned/ephemeral/deferred AND type not in infra excludes — because `is_blocked` is a derived column recomputed inside the same transaction as every edge/status write, by a fixpoint loop (repeat mark/unmark UPDATE passes until 0 rows change) that propagates a blocked parent down parent-child chains and evaluates waits-for fanout gates. Recompute self-assigns `updated_at = updated_at` to suppress ON UPDATE timestamps so derived state never plants per-node wall clock in synced rows (cited merge-conflict bug bd-578h9.19). A separate consistency checker (`issueops/blocked_consistency.go`) finds/repairs drift. Failure mode: drift if a write path forgets recompute — hence the checker. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-244-  - Atomic claim CAS (`internal/storage/issueops/claim.go:ClaimIssueInTx`, `ClaimReadyIssueInTx`): claim = conditional UPDATE keyed on `row_lock = <pre-read version>` where `row_lock` is rewritten by every path mutating status/assignee — one token detects all races instead of spelling-sensitive SQL predicates. Idempotent same-actor re-claim (agent retry support), pool pseudo-assignees claimable by anyone, typed `ClaimConflictError` carrying holder+status so callers never parse prose; refusal copy deliberately never names an unclaim command because batch agents pattern-matched it into steamrolling live claims (wy-yuclk). `ClaimReadyIssueInTx` computes the ready set and claims in the SAME transaction, walking past rows lost to racing claimers. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-245-  - Lease/heartbeat/reclaim for disposable workers (`internal/storage/issueops/lease.go`; migration `0055_move_leases_to_table.up.sql`): claims grant a 5-min-TTL lease in an ephemeral, node-local, non-replicated (`dolt_ignore`d) `leases` table — the claim's status/assignee transition commits to history, heartbeats mint zero commits. A dead worker stops heartbeating; `ReclaimExpiredLeasesInTx` snapshots the stale set, repeats the expiry predicate on delete so a concurrently rescued lease is skipped, reverts issues to open, and records per-issue recovery events. Invariant: durable truth (who owns what) is separated from coordination chatter (proof of liveness). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-246-  - Cycle prevention at edge insert (`internal/storage/issueops/dependencies.go:WouldCreateSchedulingCycleInTx`): recursive-CTE reachability over the combined scheduling edge set with UNION-distinct recursion (terminates on diamonds/cycles by unique node), plus a hierarchy check refusing blocks-edges between an issue and its own ancestor/descendant. Cycles are refused before they exist rather than detected after. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-247-  - Fanout gates & workflow templates (`types.go:WaitsForMeta`, `cmd/bd/gate.go`, `internal/molecules`): waits-for edges carry JSON gate metadata (all-children / any-children, spawner ID) so a coordinator bead unblocks when dynamically spawned children finish; standalone gate beads await human/timer/gh:run/gh:pr/bead conditions; \"molecules\" are read-only JSONL issue templates instantiated into subgraphs. [VERIFIED schema/types; gate evaluation loop only partially read — INFERRED it polls via `bd gate check`]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-248-- **What GNOSIS should steal:** (1) Single-table typed edge model where only a tiny enumerated subset participates in scheduling, with one shared predicate function per semantic set (ready-affecting, cycle-set) so a new edge type is a one-line change; (2) materialized `is_blocked` maintained by in-transaction fixpoint recompute + an independent drift checker as a repairable, testable invariant — makes ready-detection O(indexed scan) instead of graph walk per poll; (3) claim-as-CAS on a row-version token rewritten by every ownership mutation, idempotent for same-actor retries, returning typed conflict data (holder, status) — never prose — and claiming inside the same transaction that computed readiness; (4) two-plane state split: history-worthy transitions in the durable/versioned store, leases+heartbeats in an ephemeral table that never enters history, with reclaim-by-TTL as the crash-recovery path for disposable workers.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-249-- **What to avoid:** Two parallel storage stacks (\"classic\" and \"domain\") whose ready semantics are held identical only by a \"Seam A parity\" test suite — semantics defined twice is a standing drift risk they visibly pay for; and the Dolt-scale machinery (embedded-vs-server cgo split, sql-server autostart, dbproxy daemon, 60+ guarded migrations, doctor repair surface) — the claim scan is also deliberately unbounded over the ready set (noted in claim.go as a deferred fix), fine for hundreds of tasks, a footgun at scale.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-250-- **Scores:** determinism 8, durability 9, security 7, recovery 9, auditability 9, modularity 7, testability 9, windows 8, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 8\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-251-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-252-### agent-governance-toolkit — Microsoft's agent governance monorepo whose core is ACS: a stateless, deterministic, fail-closed policy decision engine (Rust) hooked into agent loops at 8 intervention points\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-253-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-254-- **Identity:** https://github.com/microsoft/agent-governance-toolkit @ 7d0cef5 (2026-08-12, shallow clone depth 1) | MIT (Microsoft); vendored-attribution `policy-engine/LICENSE.acs` is also plain MIT \"(c) 2026 responsibleai\" — no unusual riders in either [VERIFIED] | Python ~1.9k files, TS/JS ~440, C# 151, Rust 142 (the decision core), Rego 122, Go 57 | Cargo workspace + maturin/PyO3, napi-rs, .NET P/Invoke, npm, Python packaging | ~80MB, 4787 tracked files\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-255-- **Risk:** LOW (for read-only pattern mining) — no npm/pip install hooks found in the plugin or checked packages [VERIFIED]; OPA runs as external subprocess with timeout and Windows-aware binary name (`core/src/opa.rs`) [VERIFIED]; runtime network is explicit and gated: HTTPS-only manifest `extends`/bundle fetch with SHA-256 pin, size/redirect/timeout limits, fail-closed on mismatch (`manifest.rs:290-320`) [VERIFIED]; telemetry is redaction-safe by construction (content fields omitted, evidence pointer keys only, never URLs) [VERIFIED]; MCP exposure is a local stdio server with two read-only tools (`agent-governance-claude-code/server/agt-mcp.mjs`) [VERIFIED]; Dockerfile is a non-root dev container that curls toolchains at build time only [VERIFIED]. Caveats: unpinned URL `extends` is permitted (\"trusted because the host chose it\") and LLM-annotator `api_key` may be inlined in the manifest rather than env [VERIFIED].\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-256-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — The ACS decision model (host-as-PEP builds a complete JSON snapshot; stateless PDP returns one of allow/deny/warn/escalate/transform; every runtime failure collapses to deny with a reserved reason) is exactly the shape GNOSIS's Kernel/Policy Engine needs, and it is the best-evidenced fail-closed design in the survey (Quint formal model + cross-SDK conformance parity). But GNOSIS's kernel is Python/SQLite/Windows-native and should not take a Rust+maturin+OPA dependency for V1; reimplement the contract (points, verdict enum, reserved reasons, action-identity hashing, limits) in the kernel, and revisit the PyO3 SDK as a dependency candidate only if policy complexity later demands Rego/Cedar.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-257-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-258-  - Fail-closed PDP (`policy-engine/core/src/runtime.rs:evaluate_intervention_point`, `verdict.rs:Verdict::runtime_error`, `error.rs`): every failure — invalid manifest, missing path, unknown tool, annotator error, policy dispatcher error or panic (caught via `catch_unwind`), oversized/deep output, invalid verdict shape — maps to `Decision::Deny` with a reserved `runtime_error:<code>` reason; policies/annotators are forbidden from emitting that prefix (`limits.rs:reject_reserved_reason`), so a deny-by-error is always distinguishable from a policy deny. Invariant: no error path ever permits. Formally stated as `RuntimeErrorsDeny` and `ExplicitAllowRequired` in the Quint model (`tests/formal/acs_mediation.qnt`). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-259-  - Eight closed intervention points + unconfigured-point-denies (`intervention_point.rs`; `agent-os/src/agent_os/integrations/_native_adapter_runtime.py:POINT_NOT_CONFIGURED`): agent_startup/input/pre_model_call/post_model_call/pre_tool_call/post_tool_call/output/agent_shutdown as a closed enum; a request naming a point the manifest omits returns `runtime_error:intervention_point_unknown` and does NOT permit — the code comment explicitly argues a missing binding is a configuration gap, not consent, and a `post_*` block must stop already-produced output from propagating. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-260-  - Action identity & approval binding (`policy_input.rs:action_identity`, `sdk/rust/src/host/approval.rs`, `error.rs:ApprovalActionMismatch`): identity = `sha256:` over key-sorted canonical JSON of the full policy input; `input_identity` vs `enforced_identity` (recomputed over the transformed value); an `escalate` verdict routes through a host `ApprovalResolver` whose resolution is bound to the exact action hash — mismatch fails closed. Anti-TOCTOU between \"what was approved\" and \"what runs\". [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-261-  - Transform confinement (`verdict.rs:Transform::from_value`, `runtime.rs` D1.1 block): the only value-mutating decision is `transform`; its path must root at `$policy_target` (`TransformTargetForbidden` otherwise), the rewritten snapshot is re-validated against resource limits before surfacing, and adapters gate on `permits_unchanged`/`applies_to` so an unapplicable transform is treated as a deny rather than silently running the original (the \"redaction that silently doesn't redact\" failure mode is designed out). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-262-  - Least privilege via tool catalog + IFC label lattice (`tool_projection.rs`, `policy/lib/agt_ifc.rego`): a tool name resolved from the snapshot that is not declared in the manifest catalog → `ToolUnknown` → deny (deny-by-default for undeclared tools); declared tools carry `clearance`/`security_labels` projected into policy input; a stock Rego lattice (public<internal<confidential<secret) enforces no-write-down (`sink clearance must dominate max source label sensitivity`) with label propagation via `result_labels` — the host tracks provenance, the engine stays stateless. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-263-  - Evidence + conformance + resource limits (`verdict.rs:Evidence`, `tests/conformance/`, `tests/formal/`, `limits.rs`): verdicts may carry a 4KiB-bounded evidence object (artefact content-address + verification pointers) propagated to telemetry as keys-not-URLs; cross-SDK parity suites include `fail_closed_error_parity.json`; hard limits on snapshot bytes, JSON depth, annotator/policy output size, extends depth, and URL fetches. Tests themselves NOT TESTED (protocol forbids execution); their presence and content VERIFIED by reading.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-264-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-265-  1. **The snapshot→verdict contract as the Kernel's policy gate:** agents/adapters build a complete JSON snapshot per intervention point; a pure, stateless kernel function returns exactly one of `allow/deny/warn/escalate/transform`, and *every* internal failure returns deny with a reserved `runtime_error:*` reason namespace that policies may not use — this cleanly implements \"agents propose, Kernel authorizes\" and rule 29 (never suppress gates).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-266-  2. **Action-identity hashing for approvals:** sha256 over canonical (key-sorted) JSON of the evaluated action, with separate input/enforced identities, and approval resolutions bound to that hash so a NEEDS_HUMAN escalation can never authorize a different action than the one evaluated.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-267-  3. **Unconfigured-point-denies + undeclared-tool-denies:** treat a missing policy binding or an unregistered tool as a configuration gap that fails closed, never as implicit allow — directly implements GNOSIS rule 13 (deny-by-default).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-268-  4. **Formal invariants + parity fixtures as evidence:** a tiny machine-checked model (their 7 Quint invariants like RuntimeErrorsDeny) plus JSON conformance fixtures runnable against the kernel implementation — cheap to port to Python/pytest and exactly the \"evidence before consensus\" artifact GNOSIS milestones need.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-269-- **What to avoid:** (1) Toolkit sprawl — the monorepo surrounds a ~15k-line disciplined Rust core with ~19k lines of loosely-coupled Python \"agent-os\" modules (mcp_security, prompt_injection, credential_vault, sandbox, …) of much more variable rigor; steal the core contract, not the kitchen sink. (2) Regex-heuristic enforcement in the Claude Code plugin (`lib/policy.mjs`): command-pattern matching with hardcoded bypass lists (`SAFE_CLEANUP_TARGETS`, env-template regexes) and path-field name sniffing is easily evaded and drifts from the declarative manifest model — GNOSIS gates must key on structured tool args validated by the kernel, not string pattern-matching, and never hardcode bypass allowlists inside the enforcement path. Also note inline `api_key` in manifests is permitted — GNOSIS should require env/secret-store only.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-270-- **Scores:** determinism 9, durability 5, security 9, recovery 5, auditability 8, modularity 8, testability 9, windows 8, op-simplicity 4, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 9, gnosis-fit 9\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-271-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-272-### grit — AST-symbol-level lock manager coordinating parallel AI agents over git worktrees\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-273-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-274-- **Identity:** https://github.com/rtk-ai/grit @ 0f3c9d0 (2026-06-14) | Apache-2.0, no riders (copyright \"2025-2026 RTK AI\") [VERIFIED, read LICENSE] | Rust 2021 (~6.7k LOC src + shell bench scripts) | Cargo; deps: rusqlite (bundled SQLite), tree-sitter x13 grammars, aws-sdk-s3, azure_storage_blobs, tokio | ~1.8 MB worktree, 450 KB pack; no build.rs, no install hooks [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-275-- **Risk:** LOW — no telemetry, no remote code fetch, no listeners beyond a local Unix socket at `.grit/room.sock`; subprocesses are only `git`/`gh`/`kill` [VERIFIED]. Caveats: Azure access key stored plaintext in `.grit/config.json` (gitignored) [VERIFIED, `src/config.rs` + `cli/mod.rs:1494`]; `rm -rf` only in benchmark scripts on their own temp dirs [VERIFIED]. Repo ships its own CLAUDE.md pushing vendor tools (rtk/icm) — instruction-bearing content, ignore when analyzing [VERIFIED].\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-276-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the symbol-lease table (atomic check-then-set, TTL, read/write modes, intent metadata, FIFO queue) is exactly the \"semantic ownership\" layer GNOSIS Rule 15 calls for, and it is small enough (~1.5k LOC of logic) to rebuild inside the Kernel where GNOSIS owns state and gates. As a dependency it is disqualified: it does not compile on native Windows (`std::os::unix::net` un-gated in `src/room/mod.rs:3`, `kill -0` for lock liveness in `src/git/mod.rs:312`) [VERIFIED], and its locks are advisory — nothing verifies an agent only changed claimed symbols, which GNOSIS must enforce as a merge gate.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-277-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-278-  - Atomic symbol lease (`src/db/sqlite_store.rs:SqliteLockStore::try_lock`): `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction makes the expire→check→insert sequence atomic across processes; lazy TTL expiry deletes stale locks in the same transaction; compatibility matrix: write excludes all, reads share, read blocks write; same-agent re-claim updates intent/TTL idempotently. Invariant: at most one live write lock per symbol, proven by a regression test racing 16 separate connections. Failure mode: TTL expiry is not fenced — a slow agent's lease can lapse, a second agent claims the symbol, and the first can still merge later. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-279-  - Claim/grant protocol with all-or-nothing wait (`src/cli/mod.rs:cmd_claim`): ownership is *declared* per AST symbol id (`file::name`) with agent, intent, mode, TTL; on `--wait`, granted locks are released before each backoff sleep so an agent never holds partial claims while blocked (hold-and-wait avoidance); `--queue` enqueues FIFO with position-preserving re-enqueue (`db/mod.rs:enqueue`, rowid tiebreak). Failure modes: queue promotion hardcodes 600s TTL and skips worktree creation (acknowledged TODO at `cli/mod.rs:693`). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-280-  - Dependency-aware conflict semantics (`src/db/mod.rs:get_transitive_deps` + `cmd_claim --with-deps`): recursive CTE over a tree-sitter-extracted call graph; claiming `login` write-locks it and read-locks all transitive callees, so no other agent can rewrite an interface you depend on — conflict prevention *beyond* textual merge, at the semantic-dependency level. Invariant: a symbol under read lock cannot be write-claimed. Failure mode: dep graph is only rebuilt at `grit init`, so it goes stale as agents edit. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-281-  - Worktree lifecycle + serialized integration (`src/git/mod.rs:merge_worktree`): per-agent worktree `.grit/worktrees/<agent>` on branch `agent/<id>`; done = auto-commit, rebase onto current branch *inside the worktree*, then `merge --no-ff` in main, all under a PID-liveness-checked file spinlock (never steals from a live holder); refuses to merge into a dirty main tree and preserves branch+worktree on any failure so work is recoverable. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-282-  - Enforcement gap (negative finding): `cmd_done` (`src/cli/mod.rs:890`) commits `git add -A` — everything the agent touched, claimed or not — and never diffs changes against held locks (the stored per-symbol `hash` is written at init and never re-checked); locks are released and queued agents promoted even when the merge failed. Ownership is a convention agents must follow, not a gate. [VERIFIED — absence confirmed by reading `cmd_done` and grepping hash usage]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-283-  - Distributed backend fail-closed discipline (`src/db/s3_store.rs:put_lock_if_absent`): conditional PUT `If-None-Match:*`; treats only 501/NotImplemented as \"fallback allowed\" and refuses to degrade to non-atomic locking on any other error, explicitly to avoid granting two agents the same write lock. Good policy prose in code. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-284-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-285-  - A Kernel-owned `symbol_leases` SQLite table using exactly grit's shape — `(symbol_id, agent_id, intent, mode, locked_at, ttl_seconds)` PK `(symbol_id, agent_id)`, `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` check-then-set, lazy expiry in the same transaction, FK to a symbols registry so unindexed symbols are unclaimable.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-286-  - Read/write lease modes plus `--with-deps` transitive read-locking of callees (recursive CTE) as the semantic-ownership answer to Rule 15/16 — writers get exclusivity, readers pin interfaces they depend on.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-287-  - Upgrade advisory to enforced: at the GNOSIS merge gate, diff the agent branch, map hunks back to AST symbols, and fail `FAIL_POLICY` if changed-symbols ⊄ claimed-symbols — the check grit conspicuously lacks.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-288-  - The all-or-nothing claim retry (release partial grants before sleeping) and position-preserving re-enqueue (ON CONFLICT UPDATE, not INSERT OR REPLACE) as deadlock/starvation hygiene; add fencing tokens (lease epoch checked at merge time) to close grit's TTL-expiry hole.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-289-- **What to avoid:** (1) Symbol identity as bare `file::name` — two same-named methods in different classes in one file collide into one lock row (last upsert wins), and renames silently change identity; GNOSIS needs scope-qualified, hash-anchored symbol ids. (2) Releasing locks and promoting the queue even when the merge failed (`cmd_done`), which hands a contested symbol to the next agent while the previous agent's unmerged changes sit in a leftover branch — in GNOSIS the Kernel must keep the lease bound to the unresolved integration state.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-290-- **Scores:** determinism 7, durability 6, security 6, recovery 7, auditability 5, modularity 8, testability 8, windows 2, op-simplicity 8, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 6, gnosis-fit 7\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-291-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-292-### opentraces — local-first agent-trace capture: schema + append-only Git event log + evidence-graded attribution\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-293-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-294-- **Identity:** https://github.com/jayfarei/opentraces @ 2ca75f7 (2026-08-19) | MIT with verbatim rider: *\"NOTE: Data contributed through the opentraces CLI to HuggingFace Hub is licensed under CC-BY-4.0 by default, unless the contributor specifies a different license for their personal dataset repository.\"* | Python 3.10+ (core), TypeScript (pi-extension, web) | hatchling + pip/uv (uv.lock), Node for web | ~2,118 files, 36.6 MiB pack, 640 test files, ~18.7k LOC in trails+otlp alone\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-295-- **Risk:** LOW — no postinstall hooks, no eval/exec/os.system in `src/` (VERIFIED grep), no telemetry/analytics egress (VERIFIED grep); OTLP listener binds 127.0.0.1:4318 by default and warns on non-loopback (VERIFIED `capture/otlp/receiver.py`); HF publishing is opt-in behind one conservative egress predicate (VERIFIED `core/egress_clearance.py`, `unknown` → withheld). Residual notes: `pickle` accelerator caches under the Git common dir (`trails/event_index.py:328 pickle.loads`) — local-trust deserialization surface; installers rewrite Claude Code/Codex `settings.json` hooks, but only via explicit `opentraces setup <agent>` (VERIFIED `capture/claude_code/install.py`, no import-time side effects seen). [Static audit only; nothing executed — runtime behavior NOT TESTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-296-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the event-envelope, evidence-tier, and honesty-vocabulary patterns are precisely what GNOSIS's kernel-owned state/evidence layer needs, but the codebase is VERIFIED POSIX-only (`import fcntl` at module top of `core/state.py:19` and `core/bucket_store.py:25`; `os.fork()` at `cli/capture_otlp.py:538`; tmux test harness), operationally enormous (buckets/trails/capsules/datasets/workflows), and aimed at HF dataset publishing GNOSIS doesn't need. Reimplement the envelope + anchoring vocabulary natively in the GNOSIS kernel; do not depend on it.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-297-- **Signature mechanisms:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:298:  - Hash-chained append-only event log in Git refs (`src/opentraces/core/trails/models.py:TrailEvent,finalize_event` + `event_log.py:_update_event_log_ref`): every event is content-addressed (`event_id = sha256(canonical_json(material))` where material *includes* `event_sequence` + `previous_event_id`, making replay order tamper-evident) and stamped with `SCHEMA_VERSION`/`SECURITY_VERSION`/`ATTRIBUTION_VERSION`; appends land under `refs/opentraces/local/events/v1` via compare-and-swap `git update-ref <ref> <new> <expected-old>` with race detection and max 5 retries. Invariant: single total order, tamper-evident, survives any agent, no server. Failure modes: log growth (mitigated by snapshot+delta reads), pickle cache staleness (guarded by `_EVENT_CACHE_FORMAT`). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-299-  - Reconstructable-task schema (`packages/opentraces-schema/src/opentraces_schema/models.py:TraceRecord,Step,Patch,GitAnchor`): one JSONL line per session; step-based TAO loop with `ToolCall`→`Observation` joined by `source_call_id` (dangling calls get explicit `error='no_result'`), sub-agent hierarchy via `parent_step`/`subagent_trajectory_ref`, deduplicated `system_prompts` keyed by hash, `content_hash` excluding `trace_id` for dedup, `lifecycle: provisional→final`, and `generation_index` replacement-snapshot semantics (later generations are NOT stitchable supersets). `patches[]` is the authoritative output spine; `outcome.commit_sha`/`git_links` are derived projections. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-300-  - Evidence-graded trace→commit anchoring (`models.py:GitAnchor` + `core/trails/sync.py`, `capture/git/post_commit.py`): each Edit/Write is one content-addressed trace patch; a post-commit hook matches it into Git and records a 10-value `evidence_tier` (`exact_blob_hash` → `structural_symbol` → `orphan`) plus a 7-value `evidence_firmness` (`firm_observed` → `model_inferred`), with `superseded_by` sha chains for rebase/squash/amend. Invariant: attribution is never a boolean — every claim carries its evidence grade. [schema/vocab VERIFIED; hook runtime behavior INFERRED from code+docs, NOT TESTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-301-  - Context Tree substrate (`core/context_tree/models.py:ContextLayer,ContextNode` + `contract.py`): content-addressed layers of *what the model actually saw* (system / messages / tool_registry / runtime_state), each labeled `completeness: full|approximated|stub` and `capture_method: live_capture|transcript_reconstruction|proxy|otel|hardcoded_template`; `Step.context_node_id` joins record→context in one hop. Invariant: reconstruction fidelity is explicit data; \"presence never raises trust tier\". [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-302-  - OTel ingestion, not emission (`capture/otlp/receiver.py`, `mapper.py`): stdlib `ThreadingHTTPServer` OTLP/HTTP+JSON receiver (loopback:4318, JSON-only, protobuf rejected with a hint); pure-function mapper reads `gen_ai.*` semconv + `claude_code.*` attributes; raw request/response bodies paired per LLM request by `(prompt.id, event.sequence)` because request bodies carry no `request_id`; bypass-safe (receiver down ⇒ telemetry dropped, agent never blocked). Export side is lossy ATIF v1.6 + Agent Trace serializers (`publish/atif.py` docstring enumerates exactly what is dropped). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-303-  - Single egress predicate (`core/egress_clearance.py:clearance_state`): one leaf-module three-way gate (`cleared`/`not_cleared`/`unknown`) behind all three egress doors (bucket sync, dataset publish, capsule share); absence of recorded clearance is never coerced to \"safe\". [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-304-- **What GNOSIS should steal:**\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:305:  - Event envelope pattern: content-addressed event id computed over canonical JSON that *includes* sequence + previous-event pointer, with schema/security/attribution version stamps on every event — gives the GNOSIS kernel a tamper-evident, replayable log with pure stdlib + Git.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-306-  - CAS append to a hidden Git ref (`update-ref` with expected-old-value, bounded retry) as the durable, serverless event-log write primitive — directly implements GNOSIS rule 14 (Git as architecture) with multi-writer safety.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-307-  - Closed evidence vocabularies instead of booleans: grade every claim (evidence_tier × firmness), and label every reconstruction with `completeness` + `capture_method` — this operationalizes EVIDENCE BEFORE CONSENSUS and \"Memory != Truth\" as schema, not policy prose.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-308-  - Three-substrate task reconstruction: curated record (what happened) + append-only event trail (full detail, by patch_id) + context tree (what the model saw), cross-referenced by content hashes — a task is reconstructable iff all three join.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-309-- **What to avoid:** (1) POSIX-only primitives at module import time (`fcntl`, `os.fork`) — GNOSIS is Windows-native; use `msvcrt`/portalocker-style locks and threads/processes, never fork. (2) Pickle accelerator caches and unbounded per-item event fan-out — their anchor-search events needed three schema versions (v1→v2→v3, per `trails/contract.py` comments describing a ~26GB blowup) to become bounded; design summary events O(1) from day one and persist caches as JSON/SQLite.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-310-- **Scores:** determinism 9, durability 8, security 7, recovery 7, auditability 10, modularity 7, testability 8, windows 2, op-simplicity 3, unique-value 9, evidence-quality 8, gnosis-fit 8\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-311-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-312-### nono — zero-setup OS-level least-privilege sandbox for running AI agents\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-313-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-314-- **Identity:** https://github.com/nolabs-ai/nono @ bc1406e | Apache-2.0 (standard text, copyright \"Always Further, Inc\"; no riders — VERIFIED) | Rust (+C bindings)\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-315-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — the sandbox design is sound (kernel-enforced, restrictions irreversible once applied), but adoption surface includes `curl | sh` install and a remote profile registry (`registry.nono.sh`) that distributes filesystem/network policy as pullable packages; on this Windows workstation it only works under WSL2.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-316-- **Decision:** DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE — strongest candidate in the corpus for the \"run untrusted agent with least privilege\" primitive, pending Dependency Admission and WSL2 validation.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-317-- **Key mechanism:** `crates/nono/src/sandbox/mod.rs` + `linux.rs` (`PreparedLandlockSandbox::apply_raw`, `install_seccomp_notify`) — on Linux it builds a Landlock LSM ruleset via raw `SYS_landlock_create_ruleset`/`landlock_add_rule`/`landlock_restrict_self` syscalls plus seccomp-notify filters for network mediation (a supervisor arbitrates `connect`/`sendto`/`openat2` via `SeccompNotif`); on macOS it uses Seatbelt with dynamic extension issuance (`extension_issue_file`). No container/VM, so zero startup latency. [VERIFIED — read the syscall-level implementation; \"zero latency\" marketing claim SELF-REPORTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-318-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Kernel-enforced deny-by-default (Landlock + seccomp-notify supervisor) is the right enforcement floor beneath GNOSIS's policy layer — hooks alone are advisory, this is not.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-319-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-320-### agentjail — pre-execution policy guardrail (allow/deny/ask) for coding-agent tool calls\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-321-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-322-- **Identity:** https://github.com/LuD1161/agentjail @ 0c112c2 | Apache-2.0 (standard, \"agentjail contributors\"; NOTICE only credits Gryph pricing data; no riders — VERIFIED) | Go (+Rego)\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-323-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — very large trust surface for a guardrail: MITM network tunnel, credential broker/secrets vault, persistent daemon, PATH shims, self-update; macOS/Linux only; single primary maintainer despite fast release cadence.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-324-- **Decision:** ADAPT_IDEA — reimplement the hook→policy-daemon→typed-verdict pattern inside the GNOSIS Kernel/Policy Engine rather than adopting the whole stack.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-325-- **Key mechanism:** `internal/policyeval/evaluator.go` (`evaluator.Eval`) + `agentpolicy/policies/default.rego` — every agent PreToolUse hook call is normalized (command parsed into `program`/`flags`/`paths_resolved` intents via `internal/commandintent`), then evaluated against OPA Rego rules that return `{\"action\": \"deny\"|\"ask\"|\"allow\", \"rule_id\": ...}` with deny rules like `no-recursive-delete-of-protected-paths` firing on structured argv, not string matching. [VERIFIED — read evaluator and Rego; the \"~8 ms median\" latency claim is SELF-REPORTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-326-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Policy decisions over *parsed command intent* (program + flags + resolved paths) with explicit allow/ask/deny + rule_id provenance is exactly the shape GNOSIS's deny-by-default sensitive-action gate should take.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-327-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-328-### sandbox-probe — empirical measurement of a sandbox boundary from the inside\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-329-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-330-- **Identity:** https://github.com/controlplaneio/sandbox-probe @ c8a31f2 | Apache-2.0 (standard, \"control-plane.io\"; no riders — VERIFIED) | Go (59 files, single static binary)\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-331-- **Risk:** LOW for the code itself (read-and-report only, reputable vendor); operational note: its reports enumerate readable secrets paths, open sockets, and reachable hosts, so report files are themselves sensitive.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-332-- **Decision:** DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE — small, auditable verification tool; use it to prove any GNOSIS sandbox actually blocks what the policy claims (baseline-vs-sandbox diff), and to detect boundary regressions per release.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-333-- **Key mechanism:** `pkg/tasks/tasks.go` (`taskRegistry`, `taskSetRegistry`, `Task` interface) — a deterministic, sequential registry of probe tasks (`baseline_path_task`, `baseline_network_task`, `baseline_socket_task`, etc.), each emitting `Finding` objects with stable `finding_type` strings (`sensitive_readable_paths`, `unix_socket_detection`, `sandbox_detection`, ...) into a JSON report; run once unconfined, once inside the sandbox, and the diff *is* the measured boundary. [VERIFIED — read the registry code; task list matches README]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-334-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** \"Sandbox boundary = measured diff between host baseline and in-sandbox report\" turns GNOSIS's rule 24/13 assumptions into executable evidence — adopt as a mandatory pre-autonomy smoke test.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-335-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:336:### reprise — deterministic record/replay and fork-from-step for agent runs\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-337-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-338-- **Identity:** https://github.com/itsshreyasbhardwaj-design/reprise @ fbd6284 | MIT (standard 2026 Shreyas Bhardwaj; no riders — VERIFIED) | Python\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-339-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — v0.1.0, single individual maintainer, installed from Git not PyPI; recordings capture full LLM/tool payloads (secret-bearing) into portable SQLite files; the mechanism itself is offline and benign.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:340:- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the replay engine is ~50 lines of clear logic; GNOSIS should own this pattern inside its evidence/trace layer rather than depend on a pre-1.0 solo project.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:341:- **Key mechanism:** `src/reprise/replay.py` (`Replayer.respond`) + `blobs.py` (`blob_hash`, `canonical_bytes`) — requests are canonicalized to JSON and content-hashed; replay matching is occurrence-aware (the Nth identical request gets the Nth recorded response), served from a content-addressed, de-duplicated blob store in one SQLite `.reprise` file; strict mode raises `ReplayMiss` on divergence, and `fork(at=N)` replays steps <N then goes live, with `diff --gate` giving a CI exit code on divergence. [VERIFIED — read Replayer and blob hashing; \"byte-for-byte\" claim consistent with code but end-to-end determinism SELF-REPORTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-342-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Content-hash, occurrence-aware response serving from a single SQLite trace is the cheapest credible way to make GNOSIS agent failures reproducible and to turn golden runs into regression fixtures.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-343-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-344-### agent-capsule — tamper-evident, signed hash-chain receipts of agent sessions\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-345-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-346-- **Identity:** https://github.com/quantumpipes/agent-capsule @ 2043076 | Apache-2.0 (standard, \"Quantum Pipes Technologies, LLC\"; NOTICE explicitly invites independent reimplementation of the wire format — VERIFIED) | Python (PyNaCl only) + JS browser verifier\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-347-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — code is small and clean, but: adapters are deliberately fail-open (integrity gaps are silent by design), the Ed25519 key lives on the same machine as the agent (a host-level attacker can re-sign a rewritten chain), and the README's \"paste this into your agent to fetch INSTALL.md and execute it\" onboarding is precisely the remote-instruction pattern GNOSIS forbids.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-348-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — adopt the six-section capsule + hash-chain wire format (NOTICE encourages interop reimplementation) inside GNOSIS's proof-packet/evidence layer, keyed under Kernel authority instead of a local fail-open hook.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-349-- **Key mechanism:** `src/agent_capsule/core/seal.py` (`compute_hash`, `Seal`) + `core/chain.py` (`CapsuleChain.seal_and_store`/`verify`) + `core/sealing.py` (`seal_specs`) — each action becomes a capsule answering six questions (trigger, context, reasoning, authority, execution, outcome), serialized to canonical JSON, hashed with SHA3-256, Ed25519-signed over the UTF-8 hash hex string, and linked to the previous capsule's hash; verification walks the chain and reports the exact `seq` where any byte was altered. Notably it records an `authority_type` (`autonomous`/`policy`/`human_approved`) derived from the agent's permission mode. [VERIFIED — read seal/chain/sealing code]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-350-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Hash-linked, signed capsules with an explicit per-action authority field give GNOSIS \"evidence before consensus\" a cryptographic backbone — but only if sealing is fail-closed and the key is held by the Kernel, not the agent host.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-351-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-352-### catacomb — statistical regression gate over recorded, secret-redacted agent-run evidence\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-353-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-354-- **Identity:** https://github.com/realkarych/catacomb @ 91bc99c | Apache-2.0 (verbatim template — appendix copyright line left as literal `[yyyy] [name of copyright owner]`; no riders — VERIFIED) | Go\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-355-- **Risk:** LOW — plain local files, no daemon/network, CI-tested with coverage; operational caution only: `catacomb bench` spends real Claude Code money, and redaction is explicitly best-effort.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-356-- **Decision:** DEPENDENCY_CANDIDATE — directly implements GNOSIS rule 21 (\"no promotion without baseline/regression\") for Claude Code agents; evaluate via Dependency Admission before wiring into the promotion gate.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-357-- **Key mechanism:** `evidence/evidence.go` (`Meta`, env stamps, zstd-compressed run records) + `evidence/artifacts.go` (`CaptureArtifacts` routes every text artifact through `redactArtifactBytes`) + `redact/redact.go` (vendor-specific regexes — AWS/GitHub/OpenAI/Slack/JWT — plus Shannon-entropy-gated detection and fixed-point placeholders that never re-match) — each benchmark rep is stored as a self-describing evidence directory (run metadata, model/version/resource stamps, redacted transcript and artifacts), and `catacomb regress` compares baseline vs candidate groups with small-sample statistics, mapping the verdict to a CI exit code; `calibrate` A/A-splits a variant against itself to audit the gate's own sensitivity. [VERIFIED — read evidence and redact packages; statistical-method soundness SELF-REPORTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-358-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Redact-before-persist evidence directories plus a self-auditing statistical gate (A/A calibration) is the reference design for GNOSIS's promotion/regression gate — steal the calibration idea even if the tool isn't adopted.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-359-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-360----\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-361-**Corpus notes:** All six LICENSE files were read head, tail, and grep-scanned mid-body for riders (commercial restrictions, revocation, use-limits): none found; the restrictive-rider repo previously seen in this corpus is not among these six. Catacomb's LICENSE retains unfilled Apache boilerplate `[yyyy] [name of copyright owner]` — harmless but worth flagging in Dependency Admission. Nothing was executed, built, or modified; all inspection was Read/Grep/`git` metadata read-only.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-362-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-363-### spec-kit — GitHub's spec-driven development toolkit: phase-gated command templates that make the spec the source artifact and code the generated output\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-364-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-365-- **Identity:** https://github.com/github/spec-kit @ 7eee05d (2026-08-19) | MIT (standard 21-line text, no riders — VERIFIED) | Python (specify_cli) + Markdown templates\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-366-- **Risk:** LOW — official GitHub repo, MIT; the CLI only scaffolds templates into a project. Only caution: `templates/commands/*.md` support an `.specify/extensions.yml` hook mechanism that can inject third-party pre/post prompts into each phase, so any adopted template must be audited for hook wiring. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-367-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — GNOSIS already owns `gnosis-spec/`; reimplement the phase-gated pipeline (specify → clarify → plan → tasks → analyze → implement, each with structured handoffs) as Kernel task states rather than depending on the CLI.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-368-- **Key mechanism:** `spec-driven.md` (methodology) + `templates/commands/` (specify.md, clarify.md, plan.md, tasks.md, analyze.md, checklist.md, implement.md, converge.md). Each command template is a prompt contract with YAML frontmatter declaring `handoffs` to the next-phase agent (e.g. specify → speckit.plan / speckit.clarify), turning the SDLC into an explicit state machine of artifact-producing phases; `templates/*-template.md` (constitution, spec, plan, tasks, checklist) define the durable artifacts each phase must emit. The methodology doc states the inversion explicitly: \"Specifications don't serve code—code serves specifications,\" with continuous consistency validation instead of one-time gates. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-369-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Encode spec→plan→tasks as first-class Kernel-gated states with typed handoffs and per-phase artifact templates, so no Task reaches implementation without a versioned spec artifact upstream.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-370-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-371-### superpowers — Composable skill-pack methodology that force-triggers process skills (brainstorm → plan → subagent-driven TDD) before an agent writes code\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-372-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-373-- **Identity:** https://github.com/obra/superpowers @ b36e082 (2026-08-12) | MIT (standard, no riders — VERIFIED) | Markdown skills + JS/shell hooks\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-374-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — a `SessionStart` hook (`hooks/hooks.json` → `run-hook.cmd session-start`) executes code on every session start/clear/compact, and default-on telemetry loads a remote logo carrying the Superpowers version from the vendor's site (opt-out via `SUPERPOWERS_DISABLE_TELEMETRY`; README §\"Visual companion telemetry\" — VERIFIED). No project data is sent per their claim [SELF-REPORTED]. Also a commercial arm (Prime Radiant).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-375-- **Decision:** ADAPT_IDEA — do not install as a plugin dependency; port the skill-gating discipline and 2-3 specific skills' structure into GNOSIS-native skills without the hook/telemetry surface.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-376-- **Key mechanism:** `skills/using-superpowers/SKILL.md` is a meta-skill enforcing \"invoke relevant skills BEFORE any response or action,\" including a \"Red Flags\" rationalization table that pre-empts skill-skipping; the 14 skills (`brainstorming`, `writing-plans`, `subagent-driven-development`, `test-driven-development`, `systematic-debugging`, `verification-before-completion`, `using-git-worktrees`, ...) compose into a full methodology where plans are written \"for an enthusiastic junior engineer with poor taste... and an aversion to testing,\" then executed by fresh subagents per task with review between steps. Distribution is a SessionStart hook that injects the meta-skill at conversation start. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-377-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** The anti-rationalization gate (\"if a process skill applies, invoking it is not optional\") is a promotable policy pattern for GNOSIS's own skill/lesson system — procedural memory only works if invocation is enforced, not suggested.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-378-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-379-### agent-arena — Portable protocol skill for heterogeneous multi-agent debate (Claude Code × Codex) with evidence ledgers, blind judging, and preserved dissent\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-380-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-381-- **Identity:** https://github.com/zhjai/agent-arena @ f31c2a9 (2026-08-05) | MIT (standard at root and duplicated verbatim inside each skill dir, no riders — VERIFIED) | Markdown SKILL.md + YAML agent configs only, zero executable code\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-382-- **Risk:** LOW — pure instruction/protocol repo, \"not an executable orchestrator\" [VERIFIED: repo contains only .md/.yaml/.png]; residual risk is prompt-level (the protocol directs the host agent to spawn cross-agent CLI calls, which must pass through GNOSIS's own permission gates).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-383-- **Decision:** ADAPT_IDEA — its core principle is literally GNOSIS rules 10-11 (\"independent heterogeneous agents first, debate later, evidence before consensus, dissent preserved\"); adapt the mode-triage ladder and dissent-preservation output contract into the GNOSIS Claude+Codex review loop.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-384-- **Key mechanism:** `skills/agent-arena/SKILL.md` — a \"Quick Decision Gate\" selects the lightest sufficient mode from a 12-mode ladder (`solo_red_team` → `quick_panel` → `design_debate` → `evidence_arena` → ... → `full_arena`) with explicit escalation triggers (irreversible side effects, durable contracts, interdependent decisions, repeating past mistakes); the full pipeline is independent generation → claim extraction → evidence checking → critique → revision → blind judging → synthesis, with context-hygiene rules (stage packets/outputs on disk, read back only digests) and mandatory dissent preservation. Companion `deliberative-analysis` skill handles solo anti-overconfidence with escalation into the arena. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-385-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Adopt the escalation-trigger checklist plus the \"dissent preserved in the synthesis artifact\" contract as the standard shape of independent-review evidence, and its disk-staged packet discipline for keeping arena rounds out of the orchestrator's context.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-386-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-387-### TwinRouterBench — Two-track benchmark (static labels + live SWE-bench) for routing each agent step to the cheapest sufficient LLM\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-388-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-389-- **Identity:** https://github.com/CommonstackAI/TwinRouterBench @ 7cbb0de (2026-08-18) | Apache-2.0 (standard 202-line text incl. appendix, no NOTICE, no riders — VERIFIED) | Python ≥3.10, JSONL datasets, JSON configs\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-390-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — reading it is safe, but reproducing the dynamic track executes a live agent loop (`swerouter/harness/container_runner.py`, containers) against paid APIs and auto-loads `.env` from the package directory (README \"Where to run commands\") — real spend and credential-handling surface if ever run. Static track has a keyless `--backend mock` mode. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-391-- **Decision:** BENCHMARK_AGAINST — when GNOSIS implements per-step model routing, use its static question bank and downgrade-search labeling protocol as the external baseline rather than inventing a homegrown eval.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-392-- **Key mechanism:** `twinrouterbench/` CLI exposes four surfaces (`data` / `static` / `dynamic` / `swe`). The static track builds \"cheapest sufficient tier\" labels per routed step via downgrade search with mixed-model execution verification (970 labels, 520 trajectories, 5 workloads); the dynamic track routes every live call of a mini-SWE-agent across 100 held-out SWE-bench Verified cases, measuring end-to-end resolution vs realized API spend. The framing insight: routers must decide \"which model handles the next call given the full current prefix,\" not one model per whole prompt. Headline: trained router 75/100 at $25.66 vs all-Opus 74/100 at $54.73 (53.1% cost cut). [Structure VERIFIED; headline numbers SELF-REPORTED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-393-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Per-step cheapest-sufficient routing with prefix-conditioned decisions is the right cost-control model for GNOSIS's multi-agent loops, and its downgrade-search labeling is a reusable method for generating routing ground truth from GNOSIS's own task traces.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-394-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-395-### symphony — OpenAI's spec + reference daemon that turns tracker issues into isolated autonomous coding-agent runs\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-396-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-397-- **Identity:** https://github.com/openai/symphony @ 8001b52 | Apache-2.0 (LICENSE verified verbatim, standard text, no riders; NOTICE = plain Apache boilerplate, Copyright 2025 OpenAI) | Elixir (reference impl in `elixir/`), language-agnostic Markdown spec\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-398-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — self-described \"low-key engineering preview for testing in trusted environments\" [SELF-REPORTED, README.md]; it runs coding agents with a high-trust posture and no mandated sandbox (\"Mandating strong sandbox controls... \" is an explicit non-goal in SPEC.md §2.2 [VERIFIED]). Safe to study; unsafe to run as-is under GNOSIS deny-by-default rules.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-399-- **Decision:** REIMPLEMENT_PATTERN — the orchestration state machine maps almost 1:1 onto the GNOSIS Kernel's mandate (single mutation authority, claim states, failure-classified retries, bounded loops); reimplement in Python rather than adopting Elixir code.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-400-- **Key mechanism:** `SPEC.md` §7 \"Orchestration State Machine\" + `elixir/lib/symphony_elixir/orchestrator.ex` (1990-line GenServer). The orchestrator is the *only* component that mutates scheduling state; issues move through `Unclaimed → Claimed → Running/RetryQueued → Released`, run attempts have distinct terminal reasons (`Failed`/`TimedOut`/`Stalled`/`CanceledByReconciliation`) because \"retry logic and logs differ\", reconciliation runs before dispatch on every poll tick, abnormal exits get exponential backoff while normal exits get a ~1s continuation retry, and restart recovery is tracker+filesystem-driven with no durable DB. The Elixir module implements exactly this: `maybe_dispatch`, `reconcile_running_issues`, `reconcile_stalled_running_issues`, `handle_agent_down` split into normal/abnormal/input-required paths. [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-401-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Symphony independently converges on GNOSIS's core rules (single state authority, retries classified by failure type, reconcile-before-dispatch, task != session) — its state/transition vocabulary in SPEC.md §7–§8 and §14 is a ready-made checklist to diff against the GNOSIS Kernel design.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-402-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-403-### dagger — programmable CI/CD engine built on a content-addressed, lazily-evaluated call DAG\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-404-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-405-- **Identity:** https://github.com/dagger/dagger @ 0e19eba | Apache-2.0 (LICENSE verified verbatim, standard text, no riders; NOTICE adds only an informational US crypto export-control notice, not a license restriction) | Go core (`go.mod` at root), multi-language SDKs under `sdk/`\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-406-- **Risk:** MEDIUM — as a *dependency* it drags in a container runtime/BuildKit engine, a very large surface for a local Windows workstation [INFERRED from `engine/` importing BuildKit snapshot caches in `dagql/cache.go`]; as a read-only pattern source it is LOW (mature, actively maintained — HEAD commit dated 2026-08-19 [VERIFIED]).\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-407-- **Decision:** ADAPT_IDEA — adopt the content-addressed call-DAG identity scheme for GNOSIS provenance and result caching without adopting the engine.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-408-- **Key mechanism:** `dagql/call/id.go` (`type ID`, `Digest()`, `calcDigest()`) + `dagql/cache.go`. Every pipeline operation is a GraphQL call whose ID is either \"a recipe-form constructor-addressed DAG of Calls\" or a handle-form opaque cached-result reference (doc comment on `ID`); `calcDigest()` hashes the call's receiver digest, arguments, and module digest recursively, so identical recipes yield identical digests regardless of who or when they were built, and the dagql server memoizes execution per digest (cache layer in `dagql/cache.go`, SQLite-backed persistence in `cache_persistence_*.go` / `persistdb`). Determinism is therefore an *addressing* property: same inputs → same digest → cache hit instead of re-execution. [VERIFIED for the ID/digest structure; the exact cache-hit path is INFERRED from file structure, not traced end-to-end]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md:409:- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Content-address every task/tool invocation (inputs + code version + config → digest) so GNOSIS gets deterministic replay, provenance, and safe memoization for free — study `dagql/call/id.go` only, ignore the BuildKit engine.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-410-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-411-### opentelemetry-collector — vendor-neutral telemetry pipeline: typed component DAG with explicit data-mutation semantics\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-412-\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-413-- **Identity:** https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector @ fe6e69a | Apache-2.0 (LICENSE verified verbatim, standard text, no riders) | Go\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-414-- **Risk:** LOW — CNCF-governed, heavily audited, standard license; studying its internals carries no meaningful risk [VERIFIED license; governance SELF-REPORTED/community-known].\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-415-- **Decision:** ADAPT_IDEA — reuse its pipeline-graph semantics for the GNOSIS event/evidence plane (which CLAUDE.md already commits to being \"OpenTelemetry-compatible\"); the collector binary itself is unnecessary for V1.\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-416-- **Key mechanism:** `service/internal/graph/graph.go` (`Build`, `Graph.StartAll`) + `consumer` package + `internal/fanoutconsumer/logs.go`. Pipelines are compiled from config into a validated `simple.DirectedGraph` of receiver/processor/exporter/connector nodes; every consumer declares `Capabilities{MutatesData}`, and the fanout consumer clones data only for mutating consumers (readonly consumers share the original, the last mutating consumer gets it un-cloned — `fanoutconsumer/logs.go:19-53`); `StartAll` starts components in reverse topological order with receivers deliberately last, \"to avoid cases where a shared receiver is started from one pipeline and starts sending to another, not yet started pipeline\" (`graph.go:406-431`). [VERIFIED]\ndocs/research/REFERENCE_REPOSITORY_FINDINGS.md-417-- **GNOSIS takeaway:** Two directly transferable invariants for the GNOSIS event/evidence pipeline: declare mutation capability per consumer and clone only when a mutator shares data, and always start sinks before sources (reverse-topological startup) so no event is emitted into an unready pipeline.\n--\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-19-JSONL ledgers and explicit state machines, but no hashing and mutable\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-20-transition dicts.\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-21-\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-22-## Decisions\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-23-\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-24-1. **`gnosis.kernel.canonical` is the only hash primitive.**\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-25-   `canonical_json_bytes = json.dumps(sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\",\":\"),\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-26-   ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False).encode(\"utf-8\")`; every kernel\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-27-   hash is sha256 over those bytes. The contract is documented in the\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-28-   module and pinned by a re-derivable test vector. Future subsystems\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md:29:   (replay keys, action identities, approval bindings) MUST reuse it —\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-30-   introducing a second encoding requires superseding this ADR.\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-31-\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-32-2. **RunLedger events are hash-chained from a genesis anchor.** Each\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-33-   event carries `prev_hash` and `event_hash`; `append()` re-verifies the\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-34-   entire chain before extending it (never extend an unverified anchor).\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-35-   Torn final lines remain tolerated as crash evidence; interior\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-36-   malformation, sequence gaps, payload tampering, or chain breaks raise\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-37-   `LedgerCorruptionError` naming the line. Pre-chain ledgers stay\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-38-   readable as legacy evidence but refuse appends, and a chained event\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-39-   following a legacy prefix is rejected even with a plausible\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-40-   `prev_hash` (no laundering). O(n) verification per append is accepted\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-41-   at run-ledger scale; a checkpointed anchor is the designated\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-42-   optimization if that assumption breaks.\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-43-\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-44-3. **Transition tables are `MappingProxyType` of frozensets**, and\ndocs\\adr\\ADR-0004-canonical-hashing-and-chained-ledger.md-45-   `IllegalTransitionError` carries `(current, target, allowed_next)` so\ndocs\\adr\\AD